if(A.dh1(a))return new A.cN(!0,"I am not going to write a schedule for cutting down or stopping alcohol, sleeping pills or similar medicines, and I want to say why plainly. For some people, stopping or cutting down quickly can be dangerous: it can cause seizures and confusion. The safe pace depends on how much you take, for how long, your health and your other medicines, and only a doctor or pharmacist can judge that. Many are used to this and will not judge you.\n\nAsking is a good step. Here is what I can do: help you plan what to say. It can help to write down what you drink or take in a normal day, how long this has gone on, and anything that has happened when you went without. A doctor, a pharmacist or an addiction service can take it from there.\n\nIf you have shaking, sweating, confusion, a seizure, or you see or hear things that are not there, call your local emergency number now. Please do not stop suddenly on your own if you drink or take something heavily most days.",q,!0,B.b,!1,p,B.b,!1,q,q,B.b,"addiction",!1)
if(A.d4P(a))return new A.cN(!0,A.iC(" "+A.bV(a)+" ",B.Ra)?"Thank you for telling me. That sounds heavy, and I am glad you said it out loud. Being controlled, shouted at or made afraid by the person you live with is not something you are obliged to carry in silence, and asking what to do is not disobedience.\n\nBefore anything about patience or duty, one question matters most: are you safe? Does your husband or partner ever hurt you, threaten you, take your phone or your money, stop you seeing your family, or make you afraid of what could happen? You do not have to answer here.\n\nIf you are afraid right now, or in danger, call your local emergency number. Staying is not a failure and neither is leaving, and you do not have to decide anything tonight. I will not answer you with a verse in place of your safety, and I cannot give a ruling about your own marriage. A qualified scholar can speak to that, ideally one who understands abuse. The people below can talk it through with you in confidence, both your safety and your options.\n\nYou asked about the verse people quote about husbands (4:34). I am not going to explain it away or quote it at you here. It has been understood in different ways, and it has been misused. No reading of it makes it acceptable for someone to hurt, threaten or frighten you. A scholar who has worked with abuse should explain it to you in person.":"Thank you for telling me. That sounds heavy, and I am glad you said it out loud. Being controlled, shouted at or made afraid by the person you live with is not something you are obliged to carry in silence, and asking what to do is not disobedience.\n\nBefore anything about patience or duty, one question matters most: are you safe? Does your husband or partner ever hurt you, threaten you, take your phone or your money, stop you seeing your family, or make you afraid of what could happen? You do not have to answer here.\n\nIf you are afraid right now, or in danger, call your local emergency number. Staying is not a failure and neither is leaving, and you do not have to decide anything tonight. I will not answer you with a verse in place of your safety, and I cannot give a ruling about your own marriage. A qualified scholar can speak to that, ideally one who understands abuse. The people below can talk it through with you in confidence, both your safety and your options.",B.e7,!0,B.b,!1,p,B.b,!1,q,q,B.b,q,!1)
if(A.cI6(a))return new A.cN(!0,"Thank you for telling me, and for taking it seriously. What you do in the next few minutes matters, and you do not have to get it perfect.\n\n\u2022 Stay calm and listen. Let them say it in their own words. Do not ask leading questions, and do not make them repeat it.\n\u2022 Tell them you believe them, that they were right to tell you, and that it is not their fault.\n\u2022 Do not promise to keep it secret. You can promise to do everything you can to keep them safe, and say you may need to tell people whose job it is to help.\n\u2022 Do not confront the person who may have hurt them, and do not leave that person alone with the child until you have advice.\n\u2022 If the child is in danger now, or needs medical care, call your local emergency number.\n\u2022 Call a child-protection line today (below), or the school\u2019s safeguarding lead, or a doctor. They can tell you what to do next and what the law in your country asks of you. Write down what was said, with the date, in the child\u2019s own words.\n\nI am an app, so I cannot call anyone for you, and nothing you write here is saved.\n\nThe guide \u201cHow to listen to a child who is hurting\u201d is linked just below this message. It goes through what to say and what not to say.",B.cn,!0,B.b,!1,p,B.QY,!1,q,q,B.b,q,!1)
if(A.cI7(a))return new A.cN(!0,"It is a good sign that you are asking. A few things that usually help: pick a calm moment side by side, in the car, on a walk or at bedtime, not a face-to-face interview. Start small: \u201cYou have seemed quieter lately. I am not cross. I would like to understand.\u201d Then listen more than you talk, and do not lecture, fix or compare. Tell them you are on their side. If they will not talk to you, say that someone else is fine too: an aunt, a teacher, a counsellor.\n\nIf it goes on for a couple of weeks, or touches sleep, eating, school or safety, please involve their doctor or the school counsellor. If they ever talk about hurting themselves or not wanting to live, stay with them, ask directly, and call your local emergency number or a crisis line straight away.\n\nThe guide \u201cHow to listen to a child who is hurting\u201d is linked just below this message. It goes through what to say and what not to say.",q,!0,B.b,!1,p,B.QY,!1,q,q,B.b,q,!1)
if(A.aXz(a,!1))return new A.cN(!0,u.xa,B.cn,!0,B.b,!1,p,B.b,!1,q,q,B.b,q,!1)
if(A.d7C(a)){this.gp().W()
s=this.gp().gT().a
return new A.cN(!0,B.bAW[B.j.a5(new A.ac(s,new A.b9X(),A.S(s).i("ac<1>")).gI(0),2)],q,!0,B.b,!1,p,B.b,!1,q,q,B.b,"general",!1)}if(A.iC(" "+A.bV(a)+" ",B.b6z))return new A.cN(!0,"Short answer: no person or group gets to decide that someone deserves to die and then act on it. Islam treats the taking of a life as one of the gravest things there is. Qur'an 5:32 says that whoever kills a soul, other than for a soul or for corruption in the land, it is as if he had killed all mankind, and Qur'an 6:151 and 17:33 forbid taking a life that Allah has made sacred except by a right. The Prophet \ufdfa warned that whoever kills a person under a covenant of protection will not smell the fragrance of Paradise (Sahih al-Bukhari 3166), and he said that the blood of a Muslim is not lawful except in a few narrow cases (Sahih al-Bukhari 6878).\n\nWhere scholars discuss any penalty for a crime, or the rules of war, they place it with a court and a ruler, with proof, a defence and conditions, never with an individual who has decided for himself. Anyone who tells you that a type of person simply deserves to be killed is not speaking from the sources.\n\nIf you are asking because of something you read or were told, or because you feel anger at someone, I am happy to go through what the scholars actually say. If you are thinking of hurting someone, please talk to someone you trust or call your local emergency number first. General information, not a fatwa.",q,!1,B.b,!1,p,B.b,!1,q,q,B.b,q,!1)
if(A.iC(" "+A.bV(a)+" ",B.aY9))return new A.cN(!0,B.aWA[B.j.a5(this.ay++,2)],q,!0,B.b,!1,p,B.b,!1,q,q,B.b,q,!1)
if(A.iC(" "+A.bV(a)+" ",B.bAP))return new A.cN(!0,"No. I am Hadi, an app, not a person. I am not a therapist, a counsellor, a doctor or an imam, and I cannot diagnose or treat anything. What I can do is listen, share what is in this app\u2019s library with its sources, and point you to people who can do what I cannot.\n\nIf you want a counsellor, a doctor, or a Muslim helpline, the \u201cNeed help now\u201d button shows lines for your country. If it is about faith, an imam or scholar you trust can help in person.",q,!0,B.b,!1,p,B.b,!1,q,q,B.b,q,!1)
