B.arb=new A.X("ghusl",B.b_R,B.baH,"Ghusl is the full washing of the body that you must do after janabah (major impurity). Allah says: \"if you are in a state of janabah, then purify yourselves\" (Qur'an 5:6), and Qur'an 4:43.\n\nWhen it is required:\n- After sexual intercourse (even without emission).\n- After an emission of semen, awake or in a dream. A woman who has a wet dream does ghusl if she sees a discharge (Sahih al-Bukhari 282). If you wake up and see no wetness, ghusl is not required.\n- When menstruation ends, and when postnatal bleeding (nifas) ends.\n\nHow to do it (the Prophet's way): Aisha described the Prophet's bath after janabah: he washed his hands, made wudu as for prayer, ran wet fingers through the roots of his hair, poured three handfuls of water on his head, and then poured water over the rest of his body (Sahih al-Bukhari 248).\n1. Intend in your heart and say \"Bismillah\".\n2. Wash your hands and private parts, and remove any impurity from the body.\n3. Make wudu as for prayer.\n4. Pour water over your head three times, reaching the roots of the hair.\n5. Pour water over your right side, then your left, so that water reaches the whole body, including the mouth, nose and under the nails.\n\nThe obligatory minimum: wash the whole body so that no spot, including the mouth and nose, stays dry, with the intention (the Hanafi school counts rinsing the mouth and nose as obligatory and the intention as a sunnah; the Shafi'i school requires the intention). A woman need not undo her braids if water reaches the roots of her hair.\n\nA complete ghusl, with the mouth and nose rinsed, is enough to pray after: most scholars say it does the job of wudu too. The Prophet \ufdfa's own practice, shown above, was to make wudu within it.\n\nSource: Qur'an 5:6, 4:43; Sahih al-Bukhari 282, 248.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-ghusl]","ghusl",!0,"")
B.b07=s(["\\btayammum\\b","\\bdry ablution\\b","\\bpurif\\w* with (?:earth|dust|soil|sand)\\b","\\b(?:no|without|cannot find|cant find|lack of|run out of) water\\b.*\\b(?:pray|prayer|wudu|ghusl|purif\\w*)\\b","\\b(?:pray|prayer|wudu)\\b.*\\b(?:no|without) water\\b","\\bwater is not available\\b","\\bcan i pray without wudu\\b","\\bwhen is it allowed to skip wudu\\b","\\bcannot use water\\b","\\bcant use water\\b","\\bwater harms\\b"],t.s)
B.arH=new A.X("tayammum",B.b07,B.b,"Tayammum is the dry purification: using clean earth in place of wudu or ghusl when you cannot use water. Allah says: \"if you are ill or on a journey or one of you comes from the place of relieving himself or you have contacted women and do not find water, then seek clean earth and wipe over your faces and hands with it\" (Qur'an 5:6).\n\nWhen it is allowed:\n- There is no water within reach, and you have looked for it (or it is clearly not there).\n- Using water would harm you: an illness, a wound, or a danger that cold water would make it worse.\n- The water you have is needed for drinking, for yourself or others.\n\nHow to do it: the Prophet \ufdfa taught 'Ammar ibn Yasir to strike the earth with his palms, blow into them, and wipe the face and the hands with them (Sahih al-Bukhari 338).\n1. Intend and say \"Bismillah\".\n2. Strike the clean earth (soil, sand, dust, or a clean stone with dust) once with both palms and blow off the excess.\n3. Wipe your face with your hands, then wipe the backs of your hands.\n\nThe schools differ on the details: the Hanafi and Shafi'i schools say strike twice, wiping the face with the first and the arms to the elbows with the second. The Maliki school says two strikes too, with the hands to the wrists obligatory and to the elbows recommended. The Hanbali school, following the hadith above, says one strike and wipe the face and the hands to the wrists. Either way the tayammum takes the place of wudu or ghusl. It breaks like wudu does, and when water becomes available. The Prophet \ufdfa said the earth has been made a place of prayer and a means of purification for his Ummah (Sahih al-Bukhari 335).\n\nSource: Qur'an 5:6; Sahih al-Bukhari 338, 335.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-tayammum]","tayammum",!0,"")
B.bth=s(["\\bnajasa\\b","\\btypes? of (?:impurity|impurities|najasa|filth)\\b","\\bimpurit(?:y|ies)\\b","\\bwhat (?:is|are) (?:najis|najasa|impure|unclean|filthy)\\b","\\bis (?:it|this|my) (?:najis|clean|pure|impure|najasa)\\b","\\bhow (?:to|do i) (?:clean|purify|remove) (?:najasa|impurity|urine|blood)\\b","\\bare (?:dogs?|cats?|pigs?|blood|semen|urine|vomit|alcohol|wine|saliva) (?:najis|impure|pure|clean|filthy)\\b","\\b(?:cat|dog)s?\\b.*\\b(?:impure|najasa|najis|clean|unclean)\\b","\\bwhat things are najis\\b","\\b(?:urine|blood|semen|madhy|mazi|wadi|vomit)\\b.*\\b(?:on|in) (?:my )?(?:clothes?|body|prayer|floor|mat)\\b","\\bclothes?\\b.*\\b(?:urine|blood|najis|impure)\\b","\\bmadhy\\b","\\bmazi\\b","\\bwadi\\b"],t.s)
B.aqs=new A.X("najasa",B.bth,B.b,"Najasa is physical impurity that must be removed from your body, clothes and place of prayer before you pray. It is different from the impurity of janabah or hadath, which wudu and ghusl remove.\n\nThings that most scholars hold to be najis (impure):\n- Urine and stool of humans, and the thin fluid madhy and thick fluid wadi (washing the private part and the clothes it touched).\n- Flowing blood, and menstrual blood.\n- Dead animals (not killed by proper slaughter), except fish and locusts, and the flesh of swine (Qur'an 5:3).\n\nThe schools differ on:\n- Semen: najis in the Hanafi and Maliki schools; pure in the Shafi'i and Hanbali schools (though wiped away as a courtesy).\n- Alcohol: najis in most views, though some modern scholars say its impurity is spiritual, not physical.\n- A dog: the Prophet \ufdfa said that when a dog drinks from your vessel, wash it seven times (Sahih al-Bukhari 172). The Shafi'i and Hanbali schools take the dog's saliva as najis and wash seven times; the Maliki school takes the dog as pure and the washing as a ruling of worship; the Hanafi school takes the saliva as najis but differs on the number of washes. Cats are not unclean: the Prophet \ufdfa said of a cat that it is not unclean, it is one of those that go round among you (Sunan Abu Dawud 75, hasan sahih per al-Albani).\n\nHow to remove it: wash with clean water until the colour, smell and taste of the najasa are gone. If something had been spilled in a masjid, the Prophet \ufdfa told them to pour a bucket of water over it (Sahih al-Bukhari 220). A small amount that is hard to avoid is excused, within the limits each school sets.\n\nIf you are unsure whether something is najis, the default is that it is clean. Do not make yourself anxious over doubts.\n\nSource: Qur'an 5:3; Sahih al-Bukhari 172, 220; Sunan Abu Dawud 75, hasan sahih per al-Albani.\n[GO:/huda]","najasa",!0,"")
B.bmO=s(["\\bmenstrua\\w*\\b","\\bhaid\\w*\\b","\\bhayd\\b","\\bmonthly (?:cycle|bleeding|course)\\b","\\bmenses\\b","\\bnifas\\b","\\bpostnatal bleeding\\b","\\bpostpartum bleeding\\b","\\bistihadah?\\b","\\bmenopause\\b","^(?=.*\\b(?:my|her|the|a|during|on|while|with|have|having|got|get|when|if)\\b.{0,12}\\bperiods?\\b)(?=.*\\b(?:pray\\w*|fast\\w*|ramadan|quran|masjid|hajj|umrah|tawaf|husband|wife|woman|women|girl|girls|intimacy|sex|bath|ghusl|clean|pure|impure|miss\\w*|make up|touch|read|recite|enter|allowed|permissible|can|cant|cannot|should|must|do|does|ends?|stopp?ed?)\\b)"],t.s)
B.bpU=s(["\\b(?:history|era|prophets time|time of the|years|decade|century|grace period|waiting period|iddah|probation|trial period|notice period|free period|school period|class period)\\b","\\b(?:game|story|stories|app|screen)\\b","\\b(?:pill|pills|delay|postpone|medicine|tablet|injection)\\b"],t.s)
B.apR=new A.X("menstruation",B.bmO,B.bpU,"Menstruation (haid) is a natural state, not a sin or a shame, and Allah has made special allowances in it.\n\nWhile a woman has her period:\n- She does not pray, and she does not make those prayers up afterwards. A woman asked A'ishah about it and she said, in the time of the Prophet \ufdfa they were not ordered to make up the prayers they had missed (Sahih Muslim 335).\n- She does not fast, but she does make up the missed days of Ramadan later (Sahih al-Bukhari 1951).\n- She does not do tawaf of the Kaaba.\n- Intercourse is not allowed until she is clean and has bathed. Allah says: \"keep away from wives during menstruation. And do not approach them until they are pure\" (Qur'an 2:222). Everything else with her husband is allowed in the schools' view.\n- The Qur'an: most scholars say she should not touch the mushaf directly (use a cloth or a phone). Reciting it is a school matter: the Hanafi, Shafi'i and Hanbali schools restrict it, while the Maliki school and a number of other scholars allow it, especially for a student, a teacher or anyone who fears forgetting. The report usually quoted for the ban is graded weak (Jami' at-Tirmidhi 131, da'if per Darussalam). Dhikr, du'a and listening to the Qur'an are fine for everyone.\n- Entering the masjid: the schools differ (the Hanafi, Maliki and Hanbali schools forbid it; the Shafi'i school allows passing through).\n\nWhen the bleeding stops, she makes ghusl and resumes prayer and fasting. The usual length and how to count it differ by school; if the bleeding is irregular or continues beyond the usual days (istihadah, bleeding of illness), the woman is treated as pure: she makes wudu for each prayer and prays and fasts as normal. Postnatal bleeding (nifas) also stops prayer and fasting and lasts until it ends, up to about forty days in the view of most scholars.\n\nFor a particular case, ask a scholar or a trusted woman scholar; this is general information only.\n\nSource: Sahih Muslim 335; Sahih al-Bukhari 1951; Qur'an 2:222; Jami' at-Tirmidhi 131, da'if per Darussalam.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-ghusl]","period",!0,"")
B.aX8=s(["\\bistinja\\b","\\b(?:toilet|bathroom|lavatory|restroom|relieving myself|relieve myself|call of nature)\\b.*\\b(?:etiquette|manners|rules|how|clean|wash|islam|dua|sunnah|adab|qibla|hand)\\b","\\b(?:etiquette|manners|rules|adab|sunnah)\\b.*\\b(?:toilet|bathroom|lavatory|restroom|call of nature)\\b","\\bhow (?:to|do i) clean (?:myself )?after (?:the )?toilet\\b","\\bclean(?:ing)? (?:myself |yourself )?after (?:using )?(?:the )?(?:toilet|bathroom|urinat\\w*|defecat\\w*)\\b","\\btoilet paper\\b","\\bwater or (?:tissue|paper|toilet paper)\\b","\\bface (?:the )?qibla\\b.*\\b(?:toilet|urinat\\w*|relie\\w*)\\b","\\bwhich hand\\b.*\\b(?:toilet|clean|wash)\\b","\\bwhat (?:do|should) (?:i|we) (?:say|recite) (?:when|before) (?:entering|going to|going into) (?:the )?(?:toilet|bathroom|lavatory)\\b","\\burinat\\w* (?:standing|while standing)\\b","\\b(?:pee|peeing) standing\\b"],t.s)
B.asc=new A.X("istinja",B.aX8,B.b,"Cleaning yourself after the toilet is called istinja, and it is a basic part of Islamic cleanliness. The manners that come down from the Prophet \ufdfa:\n- Clean yourself with water if it is available. The Prophet \ufdfa's servant Anas used to bring a tumbler of water for this (Sahih al-Bukhari 150). Tissue, stones or similar are also enough, if used until clean and in an odd number.\n- Use the left hand for cleaning, and do not touch your private part with the right hand (Sahih al-Bukhari 153).\n- Do not face the qibla or turn your back on it in an open place when relieving yourself, and do not clean with fewer than three stones or with dung or bone (Sahih Muslim 262).\n- The du'a on entering is \"Bismillah, Allahumma inni a'udhu bika minal-khubuthi wal-khaba'ith\" (O Allah, I seek refuge in You from the male and female devils) and on leaving \"Ghufranaka\". Enter with the left foot and leave with the right.\n- Keep your body and clothes free of urine splashes; guard against drops afterwards. If you pass urine standing, make sure no spray touches you.\n\nWudu is needed afterwards for prayer, since urine and stool break wudu.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 150, 153; Sahih Muslim 262.\n[GO:/huda/guide?id=huda-wudu]","toilet",!0,"")
B.aZv=s(["\\b(?:brush\\w*|toothpaste|tooth paste|toothbrush|mouthwash|miswak|siwak|siwaak|floss\\w*|clean(?:ing)? (?:my |the )?teeth|rins\\w* (?:my |the )?mouth|gargl\\w*|dental|dentist)\\b"],t.s)
