B.atm=new A.X("downloads-offline",B.bcp,B.bgG,"Most of the app works without the internet. The Qur'an text, the stories, the guides and the duas are already inside the app. What you can download is recitation audio, so you can listen with no connection.\n\nTo do it, open Downloads (from the menu). Pick a reciter and download a surah or more; each row shows its size, whether it is waiting, downloading, paused or on your device. A few points:\n- Downloads are for the reciter you have chosen only; change the reciter on that screen if you want another.\n- \"Wi-Fi only\" is a switch so that big downloads wait for Wi-Fi and do not use mobile data. Your phone only guesses which network it is on, so check before a big download.\n- You can pause at any time and delete any download later to free the space.\n- In a web browser, downloads are kept in the browser's storage. Once you have opened the app online, the browser also keeps a copy of the app itself, so it can reopen with no internet (not in a private window, and not after you clear the site data). The Android app keeps everything on the phone and opens with no internet.\n[GO:/downloads]","downloads",!0,"")
B.buI=s(["\\b(?:kids?|children|child) (?:section|mode|corner|area|zone|page|part|feature|profile|profiles|friendly|safe)\\b","\\bfor children\\b","\\bis (?:the )?app (?:safe|suitable|ok|okay|good) for (?:kids?|children|my (?:kid|child|son|daughter))\\b","\\b(?:kids?|children) (?:in|on) (?:the )?app\\b","\\bmy (?:kid|kids|child|children|son|daughter)s? (?:use|using|play|playing|learn|learning)\\b.*\\bapp\\b","\\bparental (?:controls?|gate|check)\\b","\\bfamily wing\\b","\\bchild profiles?\\b","\\bwhere (?:are|is) (?:the )?(?:kids?|children)\\b","\\bapp for (?:kids?|children)\\b","\\bteach (?:my )?(?:kids?|children) (?:with|using|in) (?:the )?app\\b","\\bletters? for (?:kids?|children)\\b"],t.s)
B.b2K=s(["\\b(?:how to raise|parenting|discipline|hit|beat|spank|pray at what age|what age)\\b"],t.s)
B.apY=new A.X("kids-help",B.buI,B.b2K,'The For Children section has three doors: the Arabic letters (learn them one at a time), Games (play and learn), and the Stories school (stories with a lesson to talk about). On the Home screen it is the "For Children" tile. A grown-up can sit with the child.\n\nAbout privacy: the app is a general-audience app and is not directed to children under 13. In the Family Wing a parent can add child profiles that keep only a first name or nickname and the child\'s progress in the Arabic letters, and they stay on the device. No age, photo, voice, e-mail, phone number or location is asked for. Adding or removing a child sits behind a parent check. The online game tables (Ludo and Shatranj with a friend, using a six-letter code) are off for every child unless a parent switches them on. The full text is in the Privacy policy screen.\n[GO:/academy/family]',"kids",!0,"")
B.ba6=s(["\\b(?:change|switch|set|choose|pick|select|update|find|where is|how do i set)\\b.*\\b(?:my )?(?:school of (?:law|thought)|madhhab|madhab|mazhab)\\b","\\b(?:my )?(?:school of (?:law|thought)|madhhab)\\b.*\\b(?:setting|settings|menu|app|hadi|change|set|choose|select)\\b","\\bwhich school (?:does|do) (?:hadi|you|the app) follow\\b","\\bwhat madhhab (?:are you|is hadi|does hadi)\\b","\\bare you (?:hanafi|shafii|maliki|hanbali|sunni|shia)\\b","\\bset (?:to )?(?:hanafi|shafii|maliki|hanbali)\\b","\\bhanafi (?:mode|setting)\\b","\\bwhy does hadi (?:ask|say) .*school\\b"],t.s)
B.asX=new A.X("school-setting",B.ba6,B.b,'Open the Hadi screen, tap the menu in the corner and choose "Your school of law". You can pick Not sure, Hanafi, Shafi\'i, Maliki or Hanbali. It is kept on your device only.\n\nWhat it changes: where the four schools differ, I name your school\'s view first and say plainly that other schools differ. I never say another school is wrong. If you pick "Not sure", I say that the schools differ and do not pick one for you. Where I do not hold a verified text for a single school, I say that and point you to a scholar of your school.\n\nI answer from the Sunni tradition: the Qur\'an and the Sunnah, with the four Sunni schools of law. I am a study aid, not a mufti.',"madhhab setting",!0,"")
B.biP=s(["\\bis (?:my )?(?:data|privacy|information|chat|chats|conversation|conversations|messages?) (?:private|safe|secure|stored|shared|sold|saved|recorded|tracked|kept)\\b","\\b(?:privacy|privacy policy|terms of service|terms and conditions)\\b","\\bdo you (?:track|store|save|record|sell|share|collect|log) (?:my |our )?(?:data|chats?|conversations?|messages?|questions?|location)\\b","\\bwho (?:sees|reads|can see|can read) (?:my )?(?:chats?|questions?|messages?|data|conversations?)\\b","\\bdoes (?:the )?app (?:have|show|contain|run) (?:ads|adverts|advertising|advertisements)\\b","\\bany ads\\b","\\badvert\\w*\\b","\\bis (?:the )?app (?:free|paid|premium|subscription)\\b","\\bis (?:this|it) (?:free|paid)\\b","\\bhow much (?:does|do|is) (?:the )?(?:app|it) cost\\b","\\b(?:subscription|premium|pro version|in app purchase|paywall|pricing|price of the app)\\b","\\bwho (?:owns|runs|made|built|funds) (?:the |this )?app\\b","\\bwho is behind (?:the )?app\\b","\\bdelete (?:my )?(?:account|data|history|chats?|conversation|conversations)\\b","\\bclear (?:my )?(?:chat|history|conversations?|data)\\b","\\bdo i need (?:an? )?(?:account|login|sign ?up|api key)\\b","\\bsign in\\b","\\bapi key\\b","\\bis (?:it|hadi) online\\b","\\bdoes hadi (?:need|use|work) (?:the )?(?:internet|online|api|ai)\\b","\\bis (?:hadi|this) (?:an? )?(?:offline|online)\\b"],t.s)
B.brz=s(["\\boffline\\b","\\b(?:who (?:runs|owns|funds|pays for|is behind|made|built|created)|owner of|funded|funds it|funding|company behind)\\b","\\b(?:data of|big data|quran data)\\b"],t.s)
B.apJ=new A.X("privacy-free-ads",B.biP,B.brz,'What I can tell you, from the app\'s own Privacy policy screen:\n- Your settings, bookmarks, progress, reflections and saved conversations with me are stored on your device. You can clear them by clearing the app\'s data.\n- I answer from a library built into the app, which does not leave your device. Only if you choose to add your own key for an AI provider are your key and the messages of that conversation sent to that provider, under its terms. Messages that look like a personal crisis are answered on the device and are not sent or saved.\n- Accounts: if you create one, sign-in uses Firebase Authentication. You can delete your account and data from Settings: the "Delete my account" link opens the Delete account screen. What you post in the Majlis is public.\n- Prayer times, Qibla and the mosque finder use the location you give or allow; the app contacts services such as prayer-time and map services and audio servers to work.\n- The policy says: "We do not sell personal data or show advertising based on it."\n\nWhat I cannot tell you: I do not have a price list or a statement about future plans, so I will not guess whether anything will be paid or free later. The Terms and Privacy screens are the place to read the exact words; the policy there is marked as a draft.',"privacy",!0,"")
B.b4v=s(["\\bwho is wasia\\b","\\bwhat is wasia\\b","\\bwhos wasia\\b","^wasia$","^(?:tell me about )?wasia\\b","\\bis wasia (?:real|a person|an ai|a robot|ai)\\b"],t.s)
B.asZ=new A.X("wasia-who",B.b4v,B.b,"Wasia is the AI teacher of the Wasia Academy, the letter school built with love. She teaches the Arabic alphabet, reads with the child, and speaks in three voices: student, parent, and a Teachers' Corner for parents. Shall I take you there?\n[GO:/academy/gate]","academy",!0,"")
B.beI=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:wasia )?academy\\b","\\bwasia\\b","\\b(?:wasia )?academy\\b.*\\b(?:what|how|where|start|begin|open|lessons?|courses?|schools?)\\b","\\bhow (?:do|to|can) i (?:start|begin|use) (?:the )?academy\\b","\\bwhere (?:is|are) (?:the )?(?:academy|lessons|courses)\\b","\\bi want to learn (?:islam|arabic|quran|prayer)\\b.*\\bapp\\b","\\bwhat (?:lessons|courses|schools) (?:are|do you have|does the app have)\\b","\\bletter school\\b","\\bschool of letters\\b","\\bteacher in the app\\b"],t.s)
B.btH=s(["\\b(?:hifz|memori[sz]e)\\b"],t.s)
B.aq1=new A.X("academy-help",B.beI,B.btH,'The Wasia Academy is the learning part of the app. It is on the Home screen as "Wasia Academy: learn with your teacher". It opens the school of Arabic letters, with Wasia as your teacher, and the other schools for learning step by step, such as Qur\'anic Arabic, Aqeedah, Seerah, Hadith, Family life, Ramadan Mode, Hifz and Hajj and Umrah.\n\nStart with the Arabic letters if you are a beginner. A parent can set up child profiles in the Family Wing. For a quick answer rather than a course, ask me.\n[GO:/academy]',"academy",!0,"")
B.bxt=s(["\\bhow (?:does|do) (?:scoring|score|scores|points|winning) work\\b","\\bscoring\\b","\\bhow (?:do|to) (?:i )?win\\b","\\bhow does (?:the )?game (?:end|work)\\b","\\bhints?\\b.*\\bgame\\b","\\bis there a timer\\b","\\bgame (?:settings?|options?)\\b","\\bresume\\b","\\bcontinue (?:a |my |the )?(?:game|playing)\\b","\\bfinish (?:a |my |the )?game\\b","\\bunfinished game\\b","\\bgame history\\b","\\bmy (?:game )?history\\b","\\bpast games\\b","\\bprevious games\\b","\\bwhere (?:is|are) my (?:game|games|results?|history|score|scores)\\b","\\bsaved games?\\b","\\bpractice (?:with|a) friend\\b","\\bplay (?:with|against) (?:a )?friend\\b","\\bplay online\\b"],t.s)
B.bc0=s(["\\b(?:chess|shatranj|ludo|memory|trivia|timeline|names|lineage|hijrah|word|quran knowledge|stories trivia|hifz)\\b"],t.s)
B.ass=new A.X("games-general",B.bxt,B.bc0,'The Games screen lists every game that works today, in four groups: Islamic (such as Qur\'an Knowledge, Stories Trivia, the 99 Names, Lineage Challenge and the Hijrah Journey), Strategy (Shatranj, which is chess), Family (Ludo) and Words and puzzles. Ask me about a game by name, for example "how do I play Ludo", and I will tell you the rules and how to start.\n\nScoring: the quizzes show how many you got right out of the total, and after each answer you see the source. There is no timer in the quizzes. Hints are free: a hint hides two wrong answers, and you get three free hints in a game. Nothing is sold, and there are no ratings, coins or leaderboards. Memorising the Qur\'an in Hifz practice has no score.\n\nA game you started and did not finish is shown at the top of the Games screen under "Continue playing". Finished games appear in Game history (button at the bottom of the Games screen), which is kept on this device only; you can clear it there. Playing Ludo or Shatranj with a friend on another device uses a six-letter code and needs the internet and the online hall to be switched on; if it is not, the app tells you.\n[GO:/games]\n[GO:/games/history]',"games",!0,"")
B.aWs=s(["\\b(?:grade|grading|rating|status|authenticity|strength|classification)\\b.*\\b(?:hadith|saying|narration)\\b","\\b(?:hadith|saying|narration)\\b.*\\b(?:grade|graded|rated|authentic|authenticity|sahih|saheeh|hasan|daif|weak|fabricated|mawdu|forged|fake|invented|reliable|real|true|genuine)\\b","\\bis (?:this |the |that |it )?(?:hadith|saying|narration)\\b.*\\b(?:authentic|sahih|saheeh|hasan|daif|weak|fabricated|forged|fake|real|true|genuine|reliable|correct)\\b","\\bis it (?:an? )?(?:authentic|sahih|saheeh|weak|fabricated|real|genuine) (?:hadith|saying)\\b","\\bwhat is the (?:grade|status|ruling|authenticity) of\\b.*\\b(?:hadith|saying|actions are|intentions|narration)\\b","\\b(?:grade|status|rating) of (?:the )?(?:hadith|saying)\\b","\\bwhich hadith (?:are|is) (?:sahih|authentic|weak|fabricated)\\b","\\bhow (?:do|can) i (?:know|check|find out|verify) (?:if |whether )?(?:a |the |this )?(?:hadith|saying) is (?:authentic|sahih|real|true|weak)\\b"],t.s)
B.bAh=s(["\\bchina\\b","\\bhadith (?:number|no|num)\\b","\\bwhat is (?:a )?(?:sahih|hasan|daif|weak|fabricated|mawdu)\\b","\\bwhat does sahih mean\\b"],t.s)
B.aoO=new A.X("hadith-grade-ask",B.aWs,B.bAh,'I do not grade hadith myself, and I will not give a grade from memory. A grade is a scholar\'s judgment on the chain and the text, and it can differ from one scholar to another.\n\nWhat I can do:\n- For hadith from Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim, the scholars accepted the collections as sound, so a report found in them is not given a separate grade there. Where this app prints a hadith from the other four books (Abu Dawud, at-Tirmidhi, an-Nasa\'i, Ibn Majah), it shows the grade and who gave it, for example "sahih per al-Albani". Look at the source card under an answer.\n- If you ask me about one hadith by its first words or its topic, I will tell you whether it is on my shelf and show its source and grade.\n- The Hadith screen holds the Forty Hadith of Imam an-Nawawi and a daily hadith, each with its collection, number and grade where one is on file.\n- To check a saying you have seen online, search its words in the collections; if it is in none of the six books, be careful with it. A qualified scholar, or a hadith database that names the scholar who graded it, is the place to confirm it.\n\nOne example I do hold: "Actions are only by intentions" is in Sahih al-Bukhari (number 1) and in Sahih Muslim (number 1907), so it is among the most firmly established of all hadith. And "Seek knowledge even in China" is not an authentic hadith (ask me about it by name).\n[GO:/hadith]\n[GO:/search]',"hadith",!0,"")
B.bxV=s(["\\bdifference between\\b.*\\b(?:fard|farz|wajib|sunnah|nafl|mustahabb)\\b","\\b(?:fard|farz|wajib|mustahabb|nafl)\\b","\\bwhat (?:is|does) (?:fard|farz|wajib|sunnah mu?akkadah|makruh|mubah)\\b","\\bmeaning of (?:fard|farz|wajib|makruh|mubah|mustahabb|haram|halal)\\b","\\btypes? of (?:rulings?|actions?)\\b","\\bfive (?:categories|rulings)\\b","\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:difference|meaning) .*\\bsunnah\\b.*\\bnafl\\b","\\bwhat (?:are|is) (?:the )?(?:categories|kinds|levels) of (?:ruling|obligation)\\b","\\bwhat does makruh mean\\b"],t.s)
B.aOf=s(["\\b(?:app|game)\\b"],t.s)
B.apZ=new A.X("prayer-wajib-sunnah",B.bxV,B.aOf,"Islamic law (fiqh) puts every action in one of these levels:\n- Fard (obligatory): required; reward for doing it, sin for leaving it. Examples: the five daily prayers, fasting Ramadan, zakat. A communal duty (fard kifayah) is one that is lifted from everyone once enough people do it, like the funeral prayer.\n- Wajib: in the Hanafi school, a duty just below fard (established by a text that is less certain), like witr and the Eid prayer; leaving it is sinful. The other schools treat wajib and fard as the same thing, except in Hajj.\n- Sunnah (recommended): what the Prophet \ufdfa did and encouraged; rewarded, and not sinful to leave. Sunnah mu'akkadah is a sunnah he rarely left, like the sunnah before Fajr.\n- Mustahabb / nafl (praiseworthy or voluntary): extra acts, rewarded, not emphasised.\n- Mubah (permitted): neither rewarded nor sinful.\n- Makruh (disliked): better to avoid; not sinful to do.\n- Haram (forbidden): sinful to do, rewarded to avoid for Allah's sake.\n\nNote that \"sunnah\" also means the whole way of the Prophet \ufdfa (his words, actions and approvals), which is the second source after the Qur'an.","fard wajib",!0,"")
B.bg5=s(["\\b(?:chicken|hen)\\b.*\\bor\\b.*\\begg\\b","\\begg\\b.*\\bor\\b.*\\b(?:chicken|hen)\\b","\\bwhich (?:came|comes|was|is) first\\b.*\\b(?:chicken|egg)\\b"],t.s)
B.bg4=s(["\\b(?:halal|haram|eat|eating|cook|cooking|recipe|allergy|allergic|buy|price)\\b"],t.s)
B.asd=new A.X("curious-chicken-egg",B.bg5,B.bg4,"The Qur'an and the Sunnah do not say whether the chicken or the egg came first, so nobody can answer it from revelation. What we do know is that Allah is the Creator of all things (Qur'an 39:62): the chicken, the egg and everything else. It is not something we are asked to settle, and it is a fun thing to wonder about.\n\nSource: Qur'an 39:62.\n[GO:/kids]","",!0,"")
B.aWx=s(["\\bmeaning of (?:my |our |this |the )?life\\b","\\bpurpose of (?:my |our |this |the )?life\\b","\\bwhat is life (?:really )?about\\b","\\bwhy (?:do|did) (?:we|humans|people) exist\\b"],t.s)
B.aXv=s(["\\b(?:no|lost|empty|pointless|hopeless|worthless|tired of|give up|end it|dont want)\\b"],t.s)
B.apP=new A.X("curious-meaning-of-life",B.aWx,B.aXv,"The Qur'an answers this directly. Allah says He did not create the jinn and mankind except to worship Him (Qur'an 51:56). Worship in Islam is wider than prayer: it is knowing Allah, thanking Him, being honest and kind, doing good, and looking after the people and things He has put in your care. Another verse says He created death and life to test which of you is best in deed (Qur'an 67:2). So life has a purpose, and you meet it by doing your best.\n\nSource: Qur'an 51:56, 67:2.\n[GO:/quran]","",!0,"")
B.bA_=s(["\\bis (?:the )?(?:earth|world)\\s+(?:really )?(?:flat|round|a sphere|spherical)\\b","\\b(?:flat|round) earth\\b","\\bis (?:the )?earth (?:a )?(?:ball|globe)\\b"],t.s)
B.aqh=new A.X("curious-earth-shape",B.bA_,B.b,"The Qur'an does not give a lesson on the shape of the earth, and nothing in Islam requires believing it is flat. Muslim astronomers of the past knew it is round; for example, al-Biruni worked out its size about a thousand years ago. The Qur'an points to the heavens, the earth and the night and day as signs and invites us to study them (Qur'an 3:190), so you can follow the science on this with a clear conscience.\n\nSource: Qur'an 3:190.\n[GO:/quran]","",!0,"")
B.bfz=s(["\\b(?:aliens?|extraterrestrials?|ufos?)\\b","\\blife (?:on|in) (?:other planets|mars|space|the universe)\\b","\\bare we alone in the universe\\b"],t.s)
B.asM=new A.X("curious-aliens",B.bfz,B.b,"Nobody knows, and I will not guess. The Qur'an says Allah has spread living creatures through the heavens and the earth (Qur'an 42:29). Some scholars say that leaves room for life elsewhere; others say the verse is not clear on it. The Qur'an and Sunnah give no ruling and no details, and what you think about it does not affect your faith. What is certain is that Allah created beings we cannot see, such as the angels and the jinn.\n\nSource: Qur'an 42:29.\n[GO:/quran]","",!0,"")
B.b2v=s(["\\b(?:dinosaurs?|dinos?|fossils?)\\b"],t.s)
B.anJ=new A.X("curious-dinosaurs",B.b2v,B.b,"Dinosaur fossils are real, and Islam has no teaching that denies them. The Qur'an does not mention dinosaurs by name. It tells us Allah creates things we do not know (Qur'an 16:8) and invites us to travel the earth and see how He began creation (Qur'an 29:20). Many scholars say learning from fossils is welcome; Muslims only push back where a claim clearly contradicts what Allah has told us for certain. Looking at the bones of ancient creatures is a good way to wonder at His creation.\n\nSource: Qur'an 16:8, 29:20.\n[GO:/quran]","",!0,"")
B.bzn=s(["\\b(?:ghosts?|haunted|haunting|poltergeist)\\b"],t.s)
B.bds=s(["\\bholy ghost\\b"],t.s)
B.as0=new A.X("curious-ghosts",B.bzn,B.bds,"Islam does not teach that the dead come back as roaming ghosts. After death a person is in a state called barzakh, in Allah's hands, until the Day of Judgement (Qur'an 23:99-100). The Qur'an does say the jinn are real, an unseen creation with free will (Qur'an 51:56). Scholars say what people take for ghosts is often imagination, tricks of light and sound, or sometimes jinn.\n\nIf you are scared, read Ayat al-Kursi (Qur'an 2:255), say \"A'udhu billahi minash-shaytanir-rajeem\" (I seek Allah's protection from the cursed devil), and remember that Allah is the Protector. Do not try to contact spirits; that is forbidden. If the fear keeps you awake, tell a parent or someone you trust.\n\nSource: Qur'an 23:99-100, 51:56, 2:255.\n[GO:/adhkar]","",!0,"")
B.bg9=s(["^(?:please )?(?:(?:tell|give|show) me )?(?:something|a fact|a story|a fun fact)(?: that is| thats| that s)? (?:interesting|cool|fun|nice|new)\\??$","^something interesting\\??$","^(?:i am|im|i m) bored\\??$"],t.s)
B.apV=new A.X("curious-something-interesting",B.bg9,B.b,"Here are a few things worth a look: a prophet's story you may not know, the hadith of the day, or a short du'a. You can ask me, for example, \"tell me the story of Prophet Yunus\", or open the stories section.\n[GO:/stories]\n[GO:/hadith]","",!0,"")
B.bBc=s(["\\b(?:your|ur) (?:favou?rite|fav)\\b"],t.s)
B.bnK=s(["\\b(?:surah|ayah|verse|prophet|name of allah|dua|prayer|book|hadith|sahabi|companion)\\b"],t.s)
B.ard=new A.X("curious-about-hadi",B.bBc,B.bnK,"I am Hadi, the helper built into this app, not a person, so I do not have favourites the way you do. I am happy to help with questions about Islam, stories, du'as and finding things in the app. What would you like to explore?\n[GO:/stories]","",!0,"")
B.b_v=s(["\\blongest (?:surah|chapter|sura)\\b","\\b(?:which|what) (?:surah|chapter|sura) is the longest\\b","\\bbiggest (?:surah|chapter)\\b"],t.s)
B.arf=new A.X("surah-longest",B.b_v,B.b,"The longest surah is Al-Baqarah (surah 2), with 286 verses. The longest single verse in the Qur'an is also in it: the verse about writing down debts (Qur'an 2:282).\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:282.\n[GO:/quran]","longest surah",!0,"")
B.bkN=s(["\\bshortest (?:surah|chapter|sura)\\b","\\b(?:which|what) (?:surah|chapter|sura) is the shortest\\b","\\bsmallest (?:surah|chapter)\\b"],t.s)
B.arg=new A.X("surah-shortest",B.bkN,B.b,"The shortest surah is Al-Kawthar (surah 108), with three verses (Qur'an 108:1). Surahs 103 (Al-Asr) and 110 (An-Nasr) are also three verses but longer in words.\n\nSource: Qur'an 108:1.\n[GO:/quran]","shortest surah",!0,"")
B.bxx=s(["\\bfirst (?:revelation|ayah|verse|verses|surah)\\b","\\bwhat (?:was|is) (?:first )?revealed first\\b","\\bfirst thing (?:revealed|that was revealed)\\b","\\bhow did (?:the )?(?:prophets?|muhammads?) (?:revelation|wahy) begin\\b","\\bbeginning of (?:the )?(?:revelation|wahy)\\b","\\bcave of hira\\b","\\bghar e? ?hira\\b","\\bhira cave\\b","\\bwhat happened (?:at|in) (?:the )?cave\\b"],t.s)
B.arN=new A.X("revelation-first",B.bxx,B.b,"The first verses revealed were the opening of Surah Al-Alaq: \"Recite in the name of your Lord who created\" (Qur'an 96:1).\n\nAisha described how it began (Sahih al-Bukhari 3). In that report the angel came and said Read, he answered that he was not a reader, the angel pressed him three times and then recited the first verses of Al-Alaq, and he went home trembling to Khadijah, who comforted him and took him to Waraqa ibn Nawfal.\n\nThe report says that Khadijah reassured him that Allah would never disgrace him, because he kept family ties, spoke the truth, helped the weak and honoured guests. Tradition places this in the month of Ramadan, when he was about forty.\n\nSource: Qur'an 96:1; Sahih al-Bukhari 3.\n[GO:/academy/seerah]\n[GO:/quran]","first revelation",!0,"")
B.b88=s(["\\bwhat (?:is|does) taqwa\\b","\\bmeaning of taqwa\\b","\\btaqwa meaning\\b","\\bwhat is piety\\b","\\btaqwah\\b"],t.s)
B.apk=new A.X("taqwa",B.b88,B.b,"Taqwa is being conscious of Allah in a way that makes you guard yourself against what displeases Him and hold on to what pleases Him. It is often translated as God-consciousness, piety or fear of Allah, and it sits in the heart but shows in what a person does.\n\nThe Qur'an makes it the measure of worth in Allah's eyes: \"Indeed, the most noble of you in the sight of Allah is the most righteous of you\" (Qur'an 49:13). It is also what the fast is meant to build: Qur'an 2:183.\n\nSource: Qur'an 49:13, 2:183.\n[GO:/academy/aqeedah]","taqwa",!0,"")
B.bqp=s(["\\b(?:meaning|mean|means) (?:of )?in ?sha{1,2} ?a?llah\\b","\\bwhat (?:is|does) (?:the )?(?:meaning of )?in ?sha{1,2} ?a?llah\\b","\\binshaa?llah (?:meaning|mean)\\b","\\bwhat does in sha allah mean\\b"],t.s)
B.Q2=s(["\\b(?:ruling|halal|haram)\\b"],t.s)
B.ar4=new A.X("inshallah-meaning",B.bqp,B.Q2,"In shaa Allah means \"if Allah wills\". It is said when speaking about the future, because every plan depends on His will. The Qur'an teaches it: never say you will certainly do something tomorrow (Qur'an 18:23) without adding \"if Allah wills\" (Qur'an 18:24).\n\nIt is meant as humility and trust, not as a polite way of avoiding a promise. A promise made with the intention of keeping it is still a promise.\n\nSource: Qur'an 18:23, 18:24.","inshaAllah",!0,"")
B.beG=s(["\\b(?:meaning|mean|means) (?:of )?ma ?sha{1,2} ?a?llah\\b","\\bwhat (?:is|does) (?:the )?(?:meaning of )?ma ?sha{1,2} ?a?llah\\b","\\bmashaa?llah (?:meaning|mean)\\b"],t.s)
B.arE=new A.X("mashallah-meaning",B.beG,B.b,'Maa shaa Allah means "what Allah has willed (has come to be)". Muslims say it when they admire something, to remember that the good came from Allah. The Qur\'an gives the phrase in the story of the two gardens: "What Allah willed [has occurred]; there is no power except in Allah" (Qur\'an 18:39).\n\nMany scholars also teach saying it, with a blessing, when you see something that pleases you, so that admiration does not turn into envy. That practical use is a scholarly view, not a verse.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 18:39.',"maashaa Allah",!0,"")
B.b3G=s(["\\b(?:meaning|mean|means) (?:of )?astaghfirullah\\b","\\bwhat (?:is|does) (?:the )?(?:meaning of )?astaghfirullah\\b","\\bastaghfirullah (?:meaning|mean)\\b","\\bwhat does astaghfir\\w* mean\\b"],t.s)
B.apd=new A.X("astaghfirullah-meaning",B.b3G,B.b,'Astaghfirullah means "I ask Allah for forgiveness". It is the plain words of istighfar, and it is said after a sin, after a mistake, and as a regular habit. Nuh said to his people: "Ask forgiveness of your Lord" (Qur\'an 71:10).\n\nEven the Prophet \ufdfa did it constantly (Sahih al-Bukhari 6307).\n\nSource: Qur\'an 71:10; Sahih al-Bukhari 6307.',"astaghfirullah",!0,"")
B.bjR=s(["\\bwhat is (?:a |an |the )?(?:juz|jus|para|siparah|sipara)\\b","\\bwhat is (?:a |an |the )?(?:hizb|rub|manzil)\\b","\\bjuz meaning\\b","\\bhow many (?:juz|paras?|hizb)\\b","\\bwhat is a hizb\\b"],t.s)
B.aq2=new A.X("juz-hizb",B.bjR,B.b,"A juz (also called para in South Asia) is one of thirty roughly equal parts of the Qur'an, made so that it can be read in a month, one a day. The parts do not follow surah boundaries.\n\nA hizb is half a juz, so there are sixty hizb. Each hizb is split again into four quarters, which are marked in many printed copies. These divisions are for reading and memorising, and are not part of the revealed text.\n[GO:/quran]","juz and hizb",!0,"")
B.b9C=s(["\\bwhat is (?:a )?tafs?[ei]r\\b","\\btafs?[ei]r (?:meaning|mean)\\b","\\bmeaning of tafs?[ei]r\\b","\\bwhat is quran(?:ic)? (?:exegesis|commentary)\\b"],t.s)
B.at9=new A.X("tafsir-meaning",B.b9C,B.b,"Tafsir is the explanation of the Qur'an: the meaning of its words, the reasons for revelation and what its rulings and lessons are. The Qur'an says the Prophet's task included this: \"that you may make clear to the people what was sent down to them\" (Qur'an 16:44).\n\nScholars explain the Qur'an first by the Qur'an, then by the Prophet's own words, then by the Companions and the early scholars, and by the Arabic language. Well-known works include those of at-Tabari, Ibn Kathir and al-Qurtubi. A translation is not a tafsir, which is why a verse is better understood with a reliable commentary and a qualified teacher.\n\nSource: Qur'an 16:44.\n[GO:/quran]","tafsir",!0,"")
B.bxo=s(["\\bwho (?:is|was) imam muslim\\b","\\bwho (?:is|was) muslim ibn al ?hajjaj\\b","\\babout imam muslim\\b","\\bsahih muslim author\\b","\\bwho (?:wrote|compiled) sahih muslim\\b"],t.s)
B.aqv=new A.X("imam-muslim",B.bxo,B.b,'Imam Muslim ibn al-Hajjaj an-Naysaburi (died 261 AH, 875 CE) compiled Sahih Muslim, one of the two most trusted hadith collections together with Sahih al-Bukhari. He was a student of al-Bukhari and of many other scholars, and he set strict conditions for what went into his book.\n\nIn the hadith sciences the "two Sahihs" are the first of the six main collections (the Kutub as-Sittah), alongside Abu Dawud, at-Tirmidhi, an-Nasa\'i and Ibn Majah.\n[GO:/hadith]',"Imam Muslim",!0,"")
B.bcr=s(["\\bwho (?:is|was) bilal\\b","\\babout bilal\\b","\\bbilal ibn rabah\\b","\\bbilal ibn rabaah\\b","\\bfirst muadhdhin\\b","\\bfirst muezzin\\b"],t.s)
B.aot=new A.X("bilal",B.bcr,B.b,"Bilal ibn Rabah, a Companion born in Makkah to an Abyssinian family, was enslaved when he accepted Islam and was tortured for refusing to give it up. Abu Bakr bought and freed him. The Prophet \ufdfa made him the first muadhdhin, the one who gives the call to prayer.\n\nHis standing is shown in a report from Abu Hurayrah (Sahih al-Bukhari 1149).\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1149.\n[GO:/academy/seerah]","Bilal ibn Rabah",!0,"")
B.bp0=s(["\\bwho (?:is|was) hamz[ae]h?\\b","\\babout hamz[ae]h?\\b","\\bhamz[ae]h? ibn abdul? ?muttalib\\b","\\blion of allah\\b"],t.s)
B.ats=new A.X("hamza",B.bp0,B.b,"Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib was the Prophet's \ufdfa uncle and also his foster-brother and close companion. He accepted Islam in Makkah and was known for his courage, and was called the Lion of Allah. He was killed as a martyr at the Battle of Uhud in the third year after the Hijrah, and the Prophet \ufdfa grieved deeply for him.\n[GO:/academy/seerah]","Hamza ibn Abd al-Muttalib",!0,"")
B.b84=s(["\\bwhat happened (?:at|in|during) (?:the )?(?:battle of )?uhud\\b","\\bbattle of uhud\\b","^(?:tell me about )?uhud\\b","\\bwhat is uhud\\b","\\bwhy did (?:the )?muslims lose (?:at )?uhud\\b"],t.s)
B.apy=new A.X("battle-uhud",B.b84,B.b,"Uhud was fought near Madinah in the third year after the Hijrah, after the Muslims had won at Badr and Quraysh came back to take revenge. Early on the Muslims were winning, but some of the archers the Prophet \ufdfa had placed on the hill left their post, thinking the battle was won. The enemy cavalry attacked from behind, many Muslims were killed, among them Hamza, and the Prophet \ufdfa himself was wounded.\n\nThe Qur'an speaks of it and its lessons, including that the losses were a test and a cause for repentance and for steadiness: Qur'an 3:152, Qur'an 3:155.\n\nSource: Qur'an 3:152, 3:155.\n[GO:/academy/seerah]","Battle of Uhud",!0,"")
B.aRu=s(["\\bwhat happened (?:at|in|during) (?:the )?(?:battle of )?(?:the )?(?:trench|khandaq|ahzab)\\b","\\bbattle of (?:the )?(?:trench|khandaq|ahzab)\\b","^(?:tell me about )?(?:the )?(?:trench|khandaq|ahzab)\\b"],t.s)
B.apL=new A.X("battle-trench",B.aRu,B.b,"The Battle of the Trench (also called al-Ahzab, \"the confederates\") took place at Madinah in the fifth year after the Hijrah, when several tribes joined against the Muslims. On the advice of the Companion Salman al-Farisi they dug a trench across the open side of the city, which stopped the enemy's cavalry. After about a month of siege and a bitter storm the confederates withdrew.\n\nThe Qur'an recalls it: \"remember the favor of Allah upon you when armies came to [attack] you and We sent upon them a wind and armies [of angels] you did not see\" (Qur'an 33:9).\n\nSource: Qur'an 33:9.\n[GO:/academy/seerah]","Battle of the Trench",!0,"")
B.byh=s(["\\bhow did (?:the )?(?:prophet|muhammad|messenger)s? ?(?:muhammad )?(?:die|pass away|death)\\b","\\bdeath of (?:the )?(?:prophet|muhammad)\\b","\\bwhen did (?:the )?prophet (?:die|pass away)\\b","\\bhow did (?:the )?prophet die\\b"],t.s)
B.aql=new A.X("prophet-death",B.byh,B.b,"The Prophet \ufdfa died in Madinah in the eleventh year after the Hijrah, after a short illness, in the house of Aisha. She described it: Sahih al-Bukhari 4449.\n\nThe Qur'an had told him that this would come to everyone: \"Indeed, you are to die, and indeed, they are to die\" (Qur'an 39:30). During his illness Abu Bakr was asked to lead the prayers; after his death Abu Bakr told the Muslims that whoever worshipped Muhammad should know that he had died, but whoever worshipped Allah should know that Allah is ever-living and does not die.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 4449; Qur'an 39:30.\n[GO:/academy/seerah]","death of the Prophet",!0,"")
B.bA8=s(["\\bwhat is (?:a |the )?wali?ma(?:h)?\\b","\\bwali?ma(?:h)? (?:meaning|mean|feast|dinner|food)\\b","\\bwedding feast\\b","\\bmeaning of wali?ma(?:h)?\\b"],t.s)
B.ase=new A.X("walimah",B.bA8,B.b,"The walimah is the wedding feast, held after the marriage is made. It is a sunnah, and scholars teach that it should be simple and that the invitation should not be limited to the rich. The Prophet \ufdfa told Abd ar-Rahman ibn Awf to hold one: Sahih al-Bukhari 5167.\n\nAccepting an invitation to one is also encouraged, as long as nothing forbidden is going on there.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 5167.\n[GO:/academy/family-life]","walimah",!0,"")
B.aQP=s(["\\bwhat is sil(?:a|at|ah|atu|at ?ur) ?(?:al|ar|ur)? ?rahi?[mn]\\b","\\bsilat? ?(?:al|ar)? ?rahim\\b","\\b(?:how|ways) to keep (?:family )?(?:ties|relations)\\b","\\bkeep(?:ing)? (?:the )?(?:ties of )?kinship\\b","\\bmaintain (?:family )?ties\\b","\\bkeep family ties\\b"],t.s)
B.apa=new A.X("silat-ar-rahim",B.aQP,B.b,"Silat ar-rahim means keeping the ties of kinship: visiting, calling, helping and treating relatives well, even when they have not done the same. The Qur'an puts it with fearing Allah: \"and the wombs\" (Qur'an 4:1).\n\nThe Prophet \ufdfa linked it to blessing in life and provision (Sahih al-Bukhari 5986).\n\nKeeping ties does not mean putting up with abuse: if a relative is harming you, you can keep your distance and stay safe, while still wishing them well and doing what you can at a safe distance.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:1; Sahih al-Bukhari 5986.\n[GO:/academy/family-life]","keeping family ties",!0,"")
B.b_2=s(["\\bbest (?:character|manners|akhlaq|akhlaaq)\\b","\\b(?:good|best) (?:character|akhlaq) in islam\\b","\\bwhat is (?:good )?(?:akhlaq|akhlaaq|character)\\b","\\bislamic (?:manners|etiquette|character)\\b"],t.s)
B.anG=new A.X("best-character",B.b_2,B.b,"Islam puts good character at the centre of faith. Allah describes the Prophet \ufdfa: \"And indeed, you are of a great moral character\" (Qur'an 68:4). He taught that the best of people are those with the best manners (Sahih al-Bukhari 6035).\n\nHonesty, patience, kindness, keeping promises, and holding back anger are the qualities the sources keep returning to.\n\nSource: Qur'an 68:4; Sahih al-Bukhari 6035.","good character",!0,"")
B.b_C=s(["\\bwhat is (?:modesty|haya|hayaa|hayaa)\\b","\\bmodesty in islam\\b","\\bhaya(?:a)? (?:in|of|meaning)\\b"],t.s)
B.bqA=s(["\\b(?:hijab|dress|clothing|wear|cover)\\b"],t.s)
B.ao4=new A.X("modesty-haya",B.b_C,B.bqA,"Haya is a quality of shame, shyness and self-respect that stops a person from doing what is wrong or shameful and shows in speech, behaviour and the way a person treats others. It is not only about clothing. The Prophet \ufdfa said that it is a part of faith: Sahih al-Bukhari 24.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 24.","modesty (haya)",!0,"")
B.bkB=s(["\\bwhat is riya\\b","\\briy[a\u0101]\\b","\\bshowing off (?:in|my) (?:worship|prayer|good deeds)\\b","\\bdoing good (?:deeds )?to be seen\\b"],t.s)
B.ao8=new A.X("riya-showing-off",B.bkB,B.b,"Riya is doing an act of worship or a good deed to be seen and praised by people, rather than for Allah alone. The Qur'an warns of the sort of worshippers who pray in front of others and remember Allah only a little (Qur'an 4:142), and it speaks of those who show off in the prayer (Qur'an 107:4-6).\n\nThe remedy the scholars give is to renew the intention often, to hide some good deeds, and to ask Allah for sincerity. A slip into wanting praise is something to correct and ask forgiveness for, not a reason to stop doing good.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:142, 107:4.","riya",!0,"")
B.bea=s(["\\bsecret(?:ly)? charity\\b","\\bgive (?:charity|sadaqah|sadaqa|zakat) (?:secretly|in secret)\\b","\\bcharity (?:in secret|secretly)\\b","\\bhow to give (?:charity|sadaqah) (?:secretly|quietly)\\b"],t.s)
B.aoh=new A.X("charity-secretly",B.bea,B.b,"Giving in secret is praised in the Qur'an: \"If you disclose your charitable expenditures, they are good; but if you conceal them and give them to the poor, it is better for you\" (Qur'an 2:271). The Prophet \ufdfa listed among the seven people Allah shades on the Day of Judgment Sahih al-Bukhari 1423.\n\nWays to do it: give cash or an envelope to a person directly, give to a charity or an anonymous fund, or pay an invoice for someone without saying who paid. Giving openly is not wrong, and it can encourage others, but secret charity guards the sincerity of the giver.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:271; Sahih al-Bukhari 1423.\n[GO:/zakat]","secret charity",!0,"")
B.aT5=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:islamic )?(?:month of )?rajab\\b","\\brajab (?:month|meaning)\\b","\\bsacred months?\\b","\\bfour sacred months\\b"],t.s)
B.atq=new A.X("months-rajab",B.aT5,B.b,"Rajab is the seventh month of the Islamic calendar and one of the four sacred months, in which fighting is forbidden and wrongdoing is especially grave: Qur'an 9:36. The Prophet \ufdfa named them (Sahih al-Bukhari 3197).\n\nNo particular prayer or fast for Rajab is established in the authentic hadith, and reports that give fixed rewards for specific acts in Rajab are generally graded weak by hadith scholars. The Isra and Mi'raj is often connected to the 27th of Rajab, but the date is not established with certainty.\n\nSource: Qur'an 9:36; Sahih al-Bukhari 3197.\n[GO:/calendar]","Rajab and the sacred months",!0,"")
B.aOV=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:islamic )?(?:month of )?muharram\\b","\\bmuharram (?:month|meaning)\\b","\\bfirst of muharram\\b","\\bislamic new year\\b","\\bhijri new year\\b","\\bwhat is ashura\\b","\\bashura\\b"],t.s)
B.apC=new A.X("months-muharram",B.aOV,B.b,"Muharram is the first month of the Islamic (Hijri) calendar and one of the four sacred months (Qur'an 9:36). The calendar counts from the Hijrah, the migration to Madinah; it was fixed in the time of Umar, not by the Prophet \ufdfa, so the new year has no prescribed worship of its own.\n\nThe tenth of Muharram is Ashura, a day of fasting. When the Prophet \ufdfa came to Madinah he found the Jews fasting it for the day Allah saved Musa, and he said that the Muslims have a better claim to Musa and fasted it too (Sahih al-Bukhari 2004). Many scholars recommend fasting the day before or after it as well.\n\nSource: Qur'an 9:36; Sahih al-Bukhari 2004.\n[GO:/calendar]","Muharram and Ashura",!0,"")
B.baB=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:mawlid|mawlid an nabi|mawlid un nabi|milad un nabi|mawlid al nabi|maulid)\\b","\\b(?:mawlid|milad)(?: an| un| al)?(?: nabi)?\\b"],t.s)
B.arq=new A.X("mawlid",B.baB,B.b,"The Mawlid an-Nabi is the celebration of the birthday of the Prophet \ufdfa, held on or around the 12th of Rabi al-Awwal in many countries. It was not done by the Prophet \ufdfa, the Companions or the first generations, and scholars disagree about it today.\n\nSome scholars permit or encourage it as a way of sending blessings on the Prophet \ufdfa, feeding people and remembering his life, as long as nothing forbidden is done. Others hold that adding a yearly celebration that the first generations did not do is an innovation. Everyone agrees that loving him is a duty, that sending blessings on him (Qur'an 33:56) and following his sunnah are worship all year round, and that his birth date is itself recorded differently in the sources.\n\nIf this is a question for your own household, ask a qualified scholar you trust; it is a difference between recognised positions and not a question that Hadi settles.\n\nSource: Qur'an 33:56.\n[GO:/calendar]","Mawlid",!0,"")
B.bb0=s(["\\b(?:virtues?|merits?|importance|best days?) of dhul ?hijjah\\b","\\bfirst ten days of dhul ?hijjah\\b","\\b10 days of dhul ?hijjah\\b","\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:month of )?dhul ?hijjah\\b"],t.s)
B.arX=new A.X("dhul-hijjah-virtues",B.bb0,B.b,"Dhul Hijjah is the twelfth month and the month of Hajj. Its first ten days are the best days of the year for good deeds (Sahih al-Bukhari 969).\n\nPeople increase prayer, Qur'an, dhikr, charity and, if they are not on Hajj, fasting, especially on the Day of Arafah (the 9th). The 10th is Eid al-Adha.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 969.\n[GO:/calendar]","Dhul Hijjah",!0,"")
B.aXx=s(["\\bwhat is qurbani\\b","\\bqurbani (?:meaning|mean)\\b","\\bwhat is udhiyah\\b","\\budh?hiy?a(?:h)?\\b","\\beid al ?adha sacrifice\\b","\\bwhat is (?:the )?sacrifice (?:on|at|in) eid\\b"],t.s)
B.at0=new A.X("qurbani",B.aXx,B.b,"Qurbani (udhiyah) is the sacrifice of an animal on Eid al-Adha and the days after it, in memory of Ibrahim's willingness to sacrifice his son (Qur'an 37:102). Its meat is shared: some for the family, some for relatives and some for the poor.\n\nThe Qur'an says what Allah looks for in it: \"what reaches Him is piety from you\" (Qur'an 22:37). Scholars differ on whether it is obligatory (the Hanafi view, for those who have the means) or a strongly recommended sunnah (the view of most others), so ask a scholar of your school about your own case.\n\nSource: Qur'an 37:102, 22:37.\n[GO:/calendar]","qurbani",!0,"")
B.bc4=s(["\\bhow (?:to|do i|can i|should i) (?:make|do|say) du?a(?:h)? (?:properly|correctly|right|well)\\b","\\bhow to make (?:a )?dua\\b","\\betiquettes? of (?:du?a(?:h)?)\\b","\\bconditions of (?:du?a(?:h)?)\\b","\\bhow to (?:ask|call on|supplicate to) allah\\b"],t.s)
B.b01=s(["\\b(?:for|against)\\b.*\\b(?:enemy|someone|him|her)\\b"],t.s)
B.ath=new A.X("dua-properly",B.bc4,B.b01,"There is no set form for du'a; you may speak to Allah in any language and with your own words. The things that scholars teach from the sources are:\n- Begin by praising Allah and sending blessings on the Prophet \ufdfa, and end the same way.\n- Face the qibla and raise your hands if you can.\n- Ask with sincerity and certainty, and keep asking: \"Call upon Me; I will respond to you\" (Qur'an 40:60).\n- Ask for what is permitted, and not for sin or for the cutting of family ties.\n- Pick the best times: the last third of the night, between the adhan and iqamah, in sujud, and on Friday.\n- Do not become hopeless because it is not answered yet. Answers come as the thing asked for, as a harm turned away, or as a reward kept for the Hereafter.\n\nSource: Qur'an 40:60.\n[GO:/adhkar]","how to make dua",!0,"")
B.b3x=s(["\\btas[hb]ee?h(?:at)? after (?:salah|salat|prayer|namaz)\\b","\\bdhikr after (?:salah|salat|prayer|namaz)\\b","\\bwhat to say after (?:salah|salat|prayer|namaz)\\b","\\bafter (?:the )?prayer (?:dhikr|adhkar|tasbih)\\b","\\b33 (?:times )?subhanallah\\b"],t.s)
B.aol=new A.X("tasbih-after-salah",B.b3x,B.b,'After the obligatory prayer the Sunnah is to say astaghfirullah three times, then the tasbih: Subhanallah 33 times, Alhamdulillah 33 times and Allahu Akbar 33 times, then to finish the hundred with "La ilaha illallahu wahdahu la sharika lah..." The Adhkar section has the full texts in Arabic with transliteration and meaning.\n[GO:/adhkar]',"dhikr after the prayer",!0,"")
B.aZh=s(["\\bstop procrastinating (?:my )?(?:prayer|salah|salat)\\b","\\bprocrastinat\\w* (?:my |the )?(?:prayer|salah|salat)\\b","\\bkeep (?:delaying|putting off) (?:my )?(?:prayer|salah)\\b","\\bhow to pray (?:on time|regularly)\\b","\\bi (?:always )?(?:delay|put off) (?:my )?(?:prayers?|salah)\\b"],t.s)
B.aqa=new A.X("procrastinating-prayer",B.aZh,B.b,"Praying on time is a duty (Qur'an 4:103), and a delay for no reason is serious. The practical things that help most people:\n- Set the adhan notification on and treat it as the start of the prayer, not a reminder to start later.\n- Pray as soon as the time comes in, before you start another task.\n- Make wudu at once; it often breaks the habit of putting it off.\n- Pick one prayer to be solid on first, and add one more each week.\n- If you miss one, make it up as soon as you remember, and do not let one miss turn into a pile.\n\nIf this is a heavy struggle, you are not the only one, and starting again today is what counts. Ask Allah for help: no one finds the strength to pray except by His help.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:103.\n[GO:/prayer]","praying on time",!0,"")
B.bzq=s(["\\bhow (?:do|can|to) i (?:get|become|draw|grow) closer to allah\\b","\\bhow to (?:get|become|be|draw) closer to allah\\b","\\bhow to increase (?:my )?(?:iman|faith)\\b","\\bhow (?:do|can) i increase (?:my )?(?:iman|faith)\\b","\\bstrengthen (?:my )?(?:iman|faith)\\b","\\bboost (?:my )?(?:iman|faith)\\b"],t.s)
B.bhQ=s(["\\b(?:lost|losing|leaving|left)\\b"],t.s)
B.apl=new A.X("closer-to-allah",B.bzq,B.bhQ,"The sources point to a few steady things rather than one big one:\n- Pray the five prayers on time, and add some voluntary prayer, which is a way to draw near to Allah.\n- Read some Qur'an every day, even a page, with the meaning.\n- Make dhikr and ask forgiveness often.\n- Be good to people, give in charity, and keep family ties.\n- Keep company that reminds you of Allah, and leave what hardens the heart.\n\nSmall and regular is better than large and then dropped. If your faith feels low right now, that is common, and it rises and falls: Qur'an 13:28.\n\nSource: Qur'an 13:28.\n[GO:/prayer]\n[GO:/adhkar]\n[GO:/quran]","getting closer to Allah",!0,"")
B.aWn=s(["\\b(?:prophet|muhammad)s? (?:childhood|early life|youth|boyhood)\\b","\\bchildhood of (?:the )?(?:prophet|muhammad)\\b","\\bwhen was (?:the )?prophet born\\b","\\bbirth of (?:the )?prophet\\b"],t.s)
B.aqN=new A.X("prophet-childhood",B.aWn,B.b,"The Prophet Muhammad \ufdfa was born in Makkah, in the tribe of Quraysh. He was born an orphan: his father Abdullah died before his birth and his mother Aminah died when he was about six. His grandfather Abd al-Muttalib looked after him, and then his uncle Abu Talib. As a baby he was nursed in the desert by Halimah as-Sa'diyyah, as was the custom among the Arabs. As a young man he worked as a shepherd and a trader, and was known in Makkah as as-Sadiq al-Amin, the truthful, the trustworthy.\n\nThe Qur'an reminds him of it: \"Did He not find you an orphan and give [you] refuge\" (Qur'an 93:6).\n\nSource: Qur'an 93:6.\n[GO:/academy/seerah]","the Prophet's childhood",!0,"")
B.b64=s(["\\bislamic names? for (?:a )?(?:boys?|baby boys?|sons?)\\b","\\b(?:muslim|arabic) (?:baby )?(?:boy )?names?\\b","\\bnames? for (?:my )?(?:baby )?(?:boy|son)\\b","\\bboys? names? (?:in|from) islam\\b","\\bgood names? for (?:a )?boy\\b"],t.s)
B.bAQ=s(["\\b(?:girl|daughter)\\b"],t.s)
B.ask=new A.X("names-boys",B.b64,B.bAQ,'There is no list that Islam requires, but the sources give some guidance: choose a name with a good meaning, and avoid names that carry a bad meaning, or that claim a quality that belongs only to Allah. A common choice is a name of servitude to Allah, such as Abdullah or Abd ar-Rahman, a name of a prophet (Ibrahim, Yusuf, Musa, Isa, Muhammad), or of a Companion (Umar, Ali, Uthman, Bilal, Salman).\n\nIf you pick a name from the 99 Names of Allah, it is joined with Abd ("servant of"), as in Abd al-Karim. The Names of Allah section of the app lists them with their meanings.\n[GO:/games/names99]\n[GO:/academy/family-life]',"names for boys",!0,"")
B.bev=s(["\\bdo i (?:need|have|must) (?:to )?change (?:my )?name\\b","\\bchange (?:my )?name (?:after|when|if) (?:i )?(?:convert|revert|becom|embrac|accept)\\w*\\b","\\bmuslim name (?:after|when) (?:convert|revert)\\w*\\b","\\bshould i take (?:a )?muslim name\\b","\\b(?:new muslim|revert|convert) (?:name|names)\\b"],t.s)
B.aqn=new A.X("name-change-convert",B.bev,B.b,"You do not have to change your name to become a Muslim, and changing it does not make you more Muslim. What matters is whether the meaning of your name is acceptable. The scholars say a name should be changed only if its meaning is wrong, for example if it claims servitude to someone other than Allah, or means something bad.\n\nThe Prophet \ufdfa himself changed names with a bad or self-praising meaning (Sahih al-Bukhari 6192). Many new Muslims keep their given name, add an Islamic name for use in the community, or take a new one; all three are accepted. It is a personal choice, and a change of name can have legal steps in your country as well.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 6192.\n[GO:/new-muslim]","changing your name after converting",!0,"")
B.beF=s(["\\bhow (?:should|do|to|can) (?:i |a man |a husband )?(?:to )?treat (?:my |his |a )?wife\\b","\\bhusband(?:s)? (?:duties|rights|responsibilit\\w+) (?:to|towards) (?:his |a )?wife\\b","\\bbe a good husband\\b","\\bhow to be a good husband\\b","\\btreating (?:my |a )?wife\\b"],t.s)
B.bmQ=s(["\\b(?:hit|beat|strike|obey|punish)\\b"],t.s)
B.aq7=new A.X("treat-wife",B.beF,B.bmQ,"The Qur'an commands husbands: \"live with them in kindness\" (Qur'an 4:19). The Prophet \ufdfa taught the same (Sahih al-Bukhari 5186).\n\nIn practice scholars list: being patient and gentle, providing and spending on her fairly, speaking kindly, consulting her, helping at home as the Prophet \ufdfa did, protecting her dignity, and never humiliating or hurting her. Nothing in Islam gives a husband the right to hit, frighten or control his wife. If a marriage is in difficulty, a trusted family member, imam or counsellor can help.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:19; Sahih al-Bukhari 5186.\n[GO:/academy/family-life]","how to treat your wife",!0,"")
B.bk0=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:duha|dhuha|doha|chasht|zuha) (?:prayer|salah|salat|namaz)\\b","\\b(?:duha|dhuha|doha|chasht) (?:prayer|salah|salat|namaz)\\b","\\bforenoon prayer\\b","\\bhow many rakats? (?:is|in) (?:the )?(?:duha|dhuha|chasht)\\b"],t.s)
B.aou=new A.X("duha-prayer",B.bk0,B.b,"Duha is a voluntary prayer in the forenoon, from a little after sunrise until shortly before the sun reaches its highest point. It is prayed as two rak'ahs or more, in pairs. Abu Hurayrah reported that the Prophet \ufdfa advised him three things not to leave (Sahih al-Bukhari 1981).\n\nScholars differ on how often to pray it, and on how many rak'ahs at most; two is enough and shows the sunnah.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1981.\n[GO:/prayer]","Duha prayer",!0,"")
B.bmP=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?talbi?yah\\b","\\btalbi?yah (?:meaning|words|text|translation)\\b","\\blabbaik(?:a)? allahumma labbaik\\b","\\bmeaning of labbaik\\b"],t.s)
B.aoK=new A.X("talbiyah",B.bmP,B.b,'The talbiyah is what pilgrims say from the time they enter ihram for Hajj or Umrah. Ibn Umar reported the words of the Prophet \ufdfa (Sahih al-Bukhari 1549).\n\nTransliteration: Labbayk, Allahumma labbayk. Labbayka la sharika laka labbayk. Innal-hamda wan-ni\'mata laka wal-mulk. La sharika lak.\n\nMeaning: "Here I am, O Allah, here I am. Here I am, You have no partner, here I am. Indeed all praise, favour and sovereignty are Yours. You have no partner."\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1549.\n[GO:/adhkar]',"Talbiyah",!0,"")
B.b7z=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:day of )?(?:tarwi?yah|tarwiyyah)\\b","\\b(?:day|yawm) (?:al ?|of )?tarwi?yah\\b","\\b8th (?:of )?dhul ?hijjah\\b","\\beighth (?:of )?dhul ?hijjah\\b"],t.s)
B.apb=new A.X("tarwiyah",B.b7z,B.b,"The Day of Tarwiyah is the 8th of Dhul Hijjah, the first day of the main rites of Hajj. Pilgrims put on ihram (if they are doing Hajj Tamattu' they put it on again from where they are staying), go to Mina, and pray Dhuhr, Asr, Maghrib, Isha and Fajr there, shortening the four-rak'ah prayers, and stay until after sunrise on the 9th, when they go to Arafah. Those who are not on Hajj are not asked to do anything special on that day, but it is among the best days of the year (Qur'an 22:28).\n\nSource: Qur'an 22:28.\n[GO:/calendar]","Day of Tarwiyah",!0,"")
B.baT=s(["\\bwhat (?:do|should) i (?:say|recite|read) (?:in|during) (?:the )?ruku\\b","\\bruku (?:dhikr|dua|tasbih|supplication|words)\\b","\\bwhat to (?:say|recite|read) in ruku\\b","\\bwhat is said in ruku\\b","\\bsubhana rabbi?yal ?azee?m\\b"],t.s)
B.ati=new A.X("ruku-dhikr",B.baT,B.b,"In ruku (bowing) the usual words are Subhana rabbiyal-azim, which means glory be to my Lord, the Most Great, said three times or more. The wording echoes the command to exalt the name of your Lord, the Most Great (Qur'an 56:74). When you rise you say Sami'allahu liman hamidah, which means Allah hears the one who praises Him, and then Rabbana wa lakal-hamd, which means Our Lord, to You is the praise. The Prayer guide in the app has the whole prayer, with transliteration and meaning.\n\nSource: Qur'an 56:74.\n[GO:/prayer]","what to say in ruku",!0,"")
B.bt_=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:khutbah|khutba|khutbat|jumuah khutbah|friday sermon)\\b","\\bkhutbah (?:meaning|mean|in islam)\\b","\\bfriday sermon\\b"],t.s)
B.at3=new A.X("khutbah-meaning",B.bt_,B.b,"The khutbah is the sermon given by the imam before the Friday (Jumu'ah) prayer. Allah commands the Muslims to come to it and to the prayer: \"when [the adhan] is called for the prayer on the day of Jumu'ah [Friday], then proceed to the remembrance of Allah and leave trade\" (Qur'an 62:9).\n\nIt has two parts with a short sit between them: praising Allah, sending blessings on the Prophet \ufdfa, reminding the people of taqwa, and teaching something from the Qur'an and the sunnah. Worshippers listen in silence. The Eid prayers also have a khutbah, after the prayer.\n\nSource: Qur'an 62:9.\n[GO:/stories]","the khutbah",!0,"")
B.bot=s(["\\b(?:how|ways) to (?:patiently )?(?:bear|endure|handle|cope with|deal with) (?:an )?(?:illness|sickness|disease|pain)\\b","\\bpatien(?:ce|tly) (?:with|in|during) (?:an )?(?:illness|sickness|disease|pain)\\b","\\bsabr (?:in|with|during) (?:an )?(?:illness|sickness|disease)\\b","\\bhow to be patient (?:in|during|with) (?:illness|sickness)\\b"],t.s)
B.ark=new A.X("illness-patience",B.bot,B.b,"Illness is a test, and the sources are gentle about it. The Qur'an says to give good news to the patient (Qur'an 2:155). The Prophet \ufdfa said that nothing afflicts a Muslim, down to the prick of a thorn, except that Allah removes some of his sins by it (Sahih al-Bukhari 5641).\n\nPractical things: seeking treatment is part of faith, and being patient does not mean hiding pain or pretending you are not hurting. Say what you feel to Allah, keep up the prayer as far as you can (a sick person prays in the way they are able), and let people help you. If you are low or afraid, please talk to someone you trust and to your doctor; the Sakinah section and Need help now are there for the hard days.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:155; Sahih al-Bukhari 5641.\n[GO:/sakina]","patience in illness",!0,"")
B.bAv=s(["\\bhow to be (?:more )?(?:grateful|thankful)\\b","\\bgratitude in islam\\b","\\bshukr (?:in|meaning|of)\\b","\\bwhat is shukr\\b","\\bhow to (?:thank|show gratitude to) allah\\b"],t.s)
B.apT=new A.X("gratitude-shukr",B.bAv,B.b,"Shukr, gratitude, is shown by the heart (knowing every blessing is from Allah), by the tongue (saying Alhamdulillah) and by action (using the blessing in what pleases Him). The Qur'an promises: \"If you are grateful, I will surely increase you [in favor]\" (Qur'an 14:7).\n\nThe Prophet \ufdfa prayed at night until his feet swelled, and when asked why, said he wanted to be a thankful servant (Sahih al-Bukhari 1130). Some things that help: say Alhamdulillah deliberately for specific blessings, thank people, give a little in charity, and look at those who have less than you in worldly things.\n\nSource: Qur'an 14:7; Sahih al-Bukhari 1130.","gratitude (shukr)",!0,"")
B.bue=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:salat|salah|prayer of )?(?:al )?istisqa\\b","\\bistisqa\\b","\\brain prayer\\b","\\bprayer for rain\\b"],t.s)
B.aph=new A.X("istisqa",B.bue,B.b,"Istisqa is the prayer asking Allah for rain when there is drought. The Prophet \ufdfa went out to the open prayer area, asked Allah for rain, faced the qibla, turned his cloak over, and prayed two rak'ahs (Sahih al-Bukhari 1012). Scholars differ on details such as the sermon and the order, so follow the practice of your school or your local imam.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1012.\n[GO:/prayer]","Istisqa (prayer for rain)",!0,"")
B.aZa=s(["^(?:hadees|hadis|hadeeth|ahadith|ahadees)$","\\bwhat is (?:a )?(?:hadees|hadis|hadeeth|hadith)\\b","\\bwhere (?:can|do) i (?:find|read|look up) (?:a )?(?:hadith|hadees|hadeeth)\\b","\\bhow to find (?:a )?hadith\\b"],t.s)
B.b21=s(["\\b(?:about|on) (?:prayer|fasting|zakat|hajj)\\b"],t.s)
B.asu=new A.X("hadith-meaning",B.aZa,B.b21,"A hadith is a report of what the Prophet \ufdfa said, did or approved. Together with the Qur'an it is the basis of Islamic teaching. Scholars collected hadith in books and graded them for how reliable the chain of narrators is. The two most trusted collections are Sahih al-Bukhari and Sahih Muslim, followed by Sunan Abu Dawud, Jami' at-Tirmidhi, Sunan an-Nasa'i and Sunan Ibn Majah (the six books).\n\nIn the app, the Hadith section lets you read and search; every hadith Hadi quotes carries its collection and number so you can look it up, and its grade where the collection is not Bukhari or Muslim.\n[GO:/hadith]\n[GO:/sources]","what a hadith is",!0,"")
B.aP7=s(["\\bwhat (?:is|does) (?:the name )?(?:ar ?|al ?)rahman\\b","\\b(?:meaning|mean) of (?:ar ?|al ?)?rahman\\b","\\bwhat (?:is|does) (?:the name )?(?:al ?)?wadud\\b","\\b(?:meaning|mean) of (?:al ?)?wadud\\b"],t.s)
B.aUY=s(["\\b(?:surah|chapter)\\b.*\\b55\\b"],t.s)
B.asA=new A.X("names-allah-rahman-wadud",B.aP7,B.aUY,'Ar-Rahman means the Entirely Merciful, whose mercy covers all creation: "The Entirely Merciful, the Especially Merciful" (Qur\'an 1:3). Surah 55 is named after it. Al-Wadud is the Most Loving: Allah says He is "the Forgiving, the Affectionate" (Qur\'an 85:14). The Names of Allah section lists all ninety-nine with their meanings.\n\nSource: Qur\'an 1:3, 85:14.\n[GO:/games/names99]',"names of Allah: ar-Rahman and al-Wadud",!0,"")
B.b44=s(["\\bwhat is surah (?:ya ?sin|yaseen|yasin)\\b","\\bsurah (?:ya ?sin|yaseen|yasin) (?:about|meaning|summary)\\b","\\bwhat is (?:ya ?sin|yaseen) about\\b"],t.s)
B.arG=new A.X("surah-yasin",B.b44,B.b,"Surah Ya-Sin is the 36th surah, revealed in Makkah, with 83 verses (Qur'an 36:1). It is about the truth of revelation and the Prophet's message, the story of the people of a town who rejected their messengers, signs of Allah in the earth, the night and the sun, and the resurrection and the final reckoning.\n\nMany reports give it special virtues, such as reading it for the dying or for needs. Hadith scholars grade most of those reports as weak, so read it for the meaning and for the reward of reading the Qur'an in general, and do not treat any particular use as established unless a qualified scholar tells you so.\n\nSource: Qur'an 36:1.\n[GO:/quran]","Surah Ya-Sin",!0,"")
B.bdy=s(["\\bwhat is (?:the )?(?:islamic )?(?:month of )?sha ?ban\\b","\\bsha ?ban (?:month|meaning|fasting)\\b","\\bnight of bara ?at\\b","\\bshab[\\- ]?e[\\- ]?bara ?at\\b","\\b15th (?:of )?sha ?ban\\b","\\bnisf sha ?ban\\b","\\bmid ?sha ?ban\\b"],t.s)
B.as1=new A.X("months-shaban",B.bdy,B.b,"Sha'ban is the month before Ramadan. The Prophet \ufdfa fasted more in it than in any other month except Ramadan (Sahih al-Bukhari 1969).\n\nThe 15th night of Sha'ban, called the Night of Bara'at in South Asia, is where scholars differ. Some hold that reports about it strengthen each other and that praying and asking forgiveness that night is fine as a private act. Many others hold that the reports are weak, that no special prayer or gathering is established, and that fixing a ritual for it is an innovation. If you want to be safe, fast as the Prophet \ufdfa did in Sha'ban and ask forgiveness at any time of the year, and ask a qualified scholar about the rest.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 1969.\n[GO:/calendar]","Sha'ban",!0,"")
B.bgh=s(["\\b(?:dua|supplication|prayer)s?\\b.*\\b(?:anxiety|anxious|worry|worries|worried|stress|stressed|sorrow|grief|distress|distressed|sadness|sad|heavy heart|overwhelmed)\\b","\\b(?:anxiety|anxious|worry|worries|worried|stress|stressed|sorrow|grief|distress|distressed|sadness|overwhelmed)\\b.*\\b(?:dua|supplication)s?\\b","\\bwhat (?:do i|should i|can i|to) (?:say|recite|read)\\b.*\\b(?:anxious|anxiety|worried|worry|stressed|sad|distressed|overwhelmed)\\b","\\b(?:say|recite|read)\\b.*\\b(?:when|if|while)\\b.*\\b(?:anxious|worried|stressed|sad|distressed|overwhelmed)\\b"],t.s)
B.aro=new A.X("dua-anxiety",B.bgh,B.b,"The Prophet \ufdfa used to ask Allah for refuge from worry and sadness (Sahih al-Bukhari 6369):\n\n\u0627\u0644\u0644\u0651\u064e\u0647\u064f\u0645\u0651\u064e \u0625\u0650\u0646\u0651\u0650\u064a \u0623\u064e\u0639\u064f\u0648\u0630\u064f \u0628\u0650\u0643\u064e \u0645\u0650\u0646\u064e \u0627\u0644\u0652\u0647\u064e\u0645\u0651\u0650 \u0648\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0652\u062d\u064e\u0632\u064e\u0646\u0650\u060c \u0648\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0652\u0639\u064e\u062c\u0652\u0632\u0650 \u0648\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0652\u0643\u064e\u0633\u064e\u0644\u0650\u060c \u0648\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0652\u062c\u064f\u0628\u0652\u0646\u0650 \u0648\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0652\u0628\u064f\u062e\u0652\u0644\u0650\u060c \u0648\u064e\u0636\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0639\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u062f\u0651\u064e\u064a\u0652\u0646\u0650\u060c \u0648\u064e\u063a\u064e\u0644\u064e\u0628\u064e\u0629\u0650 \u0627\u0644\u0631\u0651\u0650\u062c\u064e\u0627\u0644\u0650\n\nAllahumma inni a'udhu bika minal-hammi wal-hazan, wal-'ajzi wal-kasal, wal-bukhli wal-jubn, wa dala'id-dayni wa ghalabatir-rijal.\nMeaning: O Allah, I seek refuge in You from worry and grief, from weakness and laziness, from miserliness and cowardice, from the burden of debt and from being overpowered by people.\n\nYou can say it at any time, in any language you pray in, and as often as you need. The Qur'an also speaks of hearts finding rest in remembering Allah (Qur'an 13:28).\n\nA du'a is a strong help, and it sits alongside the other help, not instead of it. If anxiety is heavy or does not lift, talking to someone you trust or to a doctor is part of looking after yourself, and it is no sign of weak faith. If you want to talk, tell me and I will stay with it.\n\nSource: Sahih al-Bukhari 6369; Qur'an 13:28.\n[GO:/adhkar]","a du'a for worry and anxiety",!0,"")
B.aYY=s(["\\b(?:wudu|ablution|wudhu|wuzu)\\b.*\\b(?:shower|showering|bath|bathing|bathroom)\\b","\\b(?:shower|showering|bath|bathing)\\b.*\\b(?:wudu|ablution|wudhu|wuzu)\\b","\\bwash(?:ing)? for prayer (?:in|while|when) (?:the )?(?:shower|bath)\\b"],t.s)
B.asG=new A.X("shower-wudu",B.aYY,B.b,"Yes, many scholars accept wudu done in the shower, as long as you do what wudu is. The Qur'an names the parts: the face, the arms to the elbows, wiping the head and the feet (Qur'an 5:6).\n\nWhat matters is that water reaches each of those parts, and that you intend it as wudu. If you shower with that intention and wash those parts, it counts for most scholars. The schools differ on details: whether the order must be kept (face, arms, head, feet), and whether the parts must be washed one right after the other without a long gap. If you follow a school, follow its rule: the safest way in any school is to do the parts in the usual order with your intention, even in the shower.\n\nMany scholars also hold that a full ghusl done with the intention to purify includes wudu, so you do not need to repeat it.\n\nIf you are unsure, the simple way is to step out of the flow, do the wudu in order under the running water, and then pray. This is the difference-of-opinion view, not a ruling on your case; for that ask a scholar of your school.\n\nSource: Qur'an 5:6.\n[GO:/academy]\n[GO:/prayer]","wudu in the shower",!0,"")
B.bfE=s(["\\b(?:how (?:to|do i)|tips? (?:to|for)) (?:sleep|sleep better|fall asleep)\\b","\\bcant sleep\\b","\\binsomnia\\b","\\b(?:sleep|sleeping) (?:well|better|routine|schedule)\\b","\\bhow much sleep\\b"],t.s)
B.bzY=s(["\\b(?:prayer|salah|dua|dhikr|adhkar|sunnah)\\b"],t.s)
B.asj=new A.X("everyday-sleep",B.bfE,B.bzY,"General advice (not medical): most adults do best on seven to nine hours, at regular times, with screens put away for the last hour, a cooler dark room, and less caffeine after midday. If you lie awake most nights for weeks, see a doctor.\n\nFrom Islam: the Qur'an counts sleep among the signs of Allah's care (Qur'an 30:23) and calls the night a time of rest (Qur'an 78:9). The Sunnah bedtime du'as and the night prayer are in the Dhikr and Du'a screen if you want to end the day that way.\n\nSource: Qur'an 30:23, 78:9.\n[GO:/adhkar]","sleep",!0,"")
B.aVz=s(["\\b(?:how (?:to|do i)|tips? (?:to|for)) (?:eat|eating) (?:healthy|healthier|better|well)\\b","\\bhealthy (?:eating|diet|food)\\b","\\beat(?:ing)? less\\b","\\boverea?ting\\b"],t.s)
B.bre=s(["\\b(?:halal|haram|ruling|fasting|ramadan)\\b"],t.s)
B.ap5=new A.X("everyday-healthy-eating",B.aVz,B.bre,"General advice (not medical): more vegetables, fruit, water and whole foods, less sugar and fried food, and smaller portions eaten slowly. A doctor or dietitian is the right person for a condition or a specific diet.\n\nFrom Islam: the Qur'an says to eat of what is lawful and good (Qur'an 2:168) and not to waste or go to excess (Qur'an 7:31). Scholars take that as a principle for food as for everything else: eat to be well, give thanks, and do not eat to excess.\n\nSource: Qur'an 2:168, 7:31.","eating well",!0,"")
B.bgq=s(["\\bhow (?:to|do i|can i) (?:save|saving) money\\b","\\bbudget(?:ing)?\\b","\\bget out of debt\\b","\\bhow (?:to|do i) manage (?:my )?money\\b","\\bspend(?:ing)? less\\b"],t.s)
B.aTH=s(["\\b(?:zakat|riba|interest|mortgage|invest\\w*|stocks?|crypto\\w*|halal|haram)\\b"],t.s)
B.apO=new A.X("everyday-money-saving",B.bgq,B.aTH,"General advice (not financial advice): write down what comes in and goes out for a month, pay the bills and debts first, set aside a fixed amount as soon as you are paid, and cut the small regular costs you do not notice. An emergency fund of a few months of costs comes before anything else.\n\nFrom Islam: the Qur'an praises those who are neither wasteful nor stingy but between the two (Qur'an 25:67), and warns against waste (Qur'an 17:26). For halal and haram in money matters, ask me about the specific question, and for zakat there is a calculator.\n\nSource: Qur'an 25:67, 17:26.\n[GO:/zakat]\n[GO:/academy/transactions]","saving money",!0,"")
B.b2a=s(["\\b(?:how (?:to|do i)|tips? (?:to|for)) (?:find|get|look for) (?:a )?(?:job|work|employment)\\b","\\bjob (?:search|hunting)\\b","\\b(?:how to|tips for) (?:do )?(?:well|better) at work\\b","\\bwork(?:place)? (?:advice|ethics)\\b","\\bhow to be (?:a )?good (?:employee|worker|boss)\\b"],t.s)
B.bi_=s(["\\b(?:halal|haram|ruling|interview)\\b"],t.s)
B.apo=new A.X("everyday-job-work",B.b2a,B.bi_,"General advice: tell people you know that you are looking, keep your CV short and specific, apply steadily rather than in bursts, and prepare for each interview by learning what the employer actually does.\n\nFrom Islam: work done honestly is worship, and the Qur'an tells believers to spread out in the land and seek Allah's bounty after prayer (Qur'an 62:10). Do the work you are paid for properly, be honest about your hours, and be fair to those who work for you. If a job is in a doubtful area, ask me about that specific work.\n\nSource: Qur'an 62:10.\n[GO:/academy/transactions]","work and jobs",!0,"")
B.bs8=s(["\\bhow (?:to|do i|can i) (?:study|learn|revise|focus|concentrate)\\b","\\bstudy (?:tips|habits|routine|better)\\b","\\bexam (?:stress|tips|prep|preparation)\\b","\\bhow to memori[sz]e\\b"],t.s)
B.b6g=s(["\\b(?:quran|quran|hifz|surah|arabic|english|french|spanish|german|urdu|language|languages)\\b"],t.s)
B.aoC=new A.X("everyday-study",B.bs8,B.b6g,"General advice: study in short blocks with breaks, test yourself instead of rereading, sleep before an exam rather than cramming all night, and put the phone in another room.\n\nFrom Islam: the Qur'an teaches a du'a for more knowledge (Qur'an 20:114), and asks whether those who know are equal to those who do not (Qur'an 39:9). Ask Allah for ease before you start, then do the work: both are part of it.\n\nSource: Qur'an 20:114, 39:9.\n[GO:/academy/hifz]","studying",!0,"")
B.beB=s(["\\b(?:how (?:to|do i)|tips? (?:to|for)) (?:raise|parent|discipline) (?:my )?(?:kids?|children|child|toddler|teen\\w*)\\b","\\bparenting (?:advice|tips|help)\\b","\\bmy (?:kid|child|son|daughter|toddler|teenager) (?:wont|will not|refuses|is not listening)\\b","\\btantrums?\\b"],t.s)
B.buU=s(["\\b(?:abuse|hit|beat|hurt)\\w*\\b"],t.s)
B.ate=new A.X("everyday-parenting",B.beB,B.buU,"General advice: be calm and consistent, set a few clear rules and keep them, explain the reason, notice good behaviour more than bad, and give regular one to one time. Children copy what they see more than what they are told. For a serious or lasting difficulty, a doctor or a family counsellor can help.\n\nFrom Islam: the Qur'an tells believers to guard themselves and their families (Qur'an 66:6), and a parent's du'a for their children is part of the work (Qur'an 25:74). The Family section of the Academy has more.\n\nSource: Qur'an 66:6, 25:74.\n[GO:/academy/family]\n[GO:/kids]","parenting",!0,"")
B.bfr=s(["\\b(?:how (?:to|do i)|tips? (?:to|for)) (?:treat|deal with|be good to) (?:my )?(?:neighbou?rs?|guests?|co-?workers?|colleagues?)\\b","\\bgood manners\\b","\\bhow (?:to|do i) be polite\\b","\\bneighbou?rs? (?:rights|noise|problem)\\b"],t.s)
B.anH=new A.X("everyday-neighbours-manners",B.bfr,B.Q2,"General advice: greet them, keep noise down at night, return what you borrow, bring a dish on occasions, and speak to them privately and kindly before a small problem becomes a big one.\n\nFrom Islam: the Qur'an puts kindness to the neighbour next to kindness to parents and relatives (Qur'an 4:36), and tells us to speak to people well (Qur'an 2:83). The Character section of the Academy goes further.\n\nSource: Qur'an 4:36, 2:83.\n[GO:/academy/character]","neighbours and manners",!0,"")
B.bdK=s(["\\b(?:how (?:to|do i)|tips? (?:to|for)) (?:manage|use|organi[sz]e|plan) (?:my )?(?:time|day)\\b","\\btime management\\b","\\bbe more productive\\b","\\bprocrastinat\\w*\\b"],t.s)
B.bcm=s(["\\b(?:prayer|salah|salat)\\b"],t.s)
B.are=new A.X("everyday-time-management",B.bdK,B.bcm,"General advice: choose the one or two things that matter most each day and do them first, keep a short list, and protect a block of time without interruptions. The five prayers give a day its natural frame: plan around them rather than squeezing them in.\n\nFrom Islam: the Qur'an swears by time and says people are at a loss except those who believe, do good, and urge one another to truth and patience (Qur'an 103:1). Prayer Times shows when each prayer begins where you are.\n\nSource: Qur'an 103:1.\n[GO:/prayer]","using time well",!0,"")
B.b2L=s(["\\b(?:how (?:to|do i)|tips? (?:to|for)) (?:travel|plan a trip|pack)\\b","\\btravel(?:l?ing)? tips\\b","\\bwhat to pack\\b","\\bplanning a (?:trip|holiday|vacation)\\b"],t.s)
B.bcR=s(["\\b(?:hajj|umrah|qasr|shorten\\w*|combine\\w*|jam)\\b"],t.s)
B.anF=new A.X("everyday-travel",B.b2L,B.bcR,"General advice: check the weather, keep copies of your documents, carry a charger and some water, and tell someone your plan. Look up where you will stay before you arrive.\n\nFor a Muslim traveller the app helps with the prayer side: Prayer Times and Qibla work from your location, and the Mosques screen shows places to pray nearby. If you want the rules for shortening or joining prayers on a journey, ask me that directly.\n[GO:/prayer]\n[GO:/qibla]\n[GO:/masajid]","travelling",!0,"")
B.brN=s(["\\blast (?:surah|sura|chapter) (?:that )?(?:was )?revealed\\b","\\b(?:which|what) (?:surah|sura|chapter) was (?:the )?last (?:revealed|to be revealed)\\b","\\blast (?:ayah|verse) (?:that )?(?:was )?revealed\\b","\\blast thing (?:that )?(?:was )?revealed\\b"],t.s)
B.ar6=new A.X("last-surah-revealed",B.brN,B.b,"Scholars give more than one answer, because the reports differ.\n\nFor the last complete surah, the view most often given is An-Nasr (surah 110), reported from Ibn Abbas (Qur'an 110:1). For the last verses, the reports name different ones, and the ones most often mentioned are the verse that warns of the Day you are returned to Allah (Qur'an 2:281), and the verse that says the religion is completed (Qur'an 5:3), which was revealed at the farewell pilgrimage.\n\nSo there is no single agreed answer, and I would not state one as settled. A tafsir book by a recognised scholar lays out the reports.\n\nSource: Qur'an 110:1, 2:281, 5:3.\n[GO:/quran]","last revelation",!0,"")
B.b_V=s(["\\b(?:(?:tell|give) me )?something (?:inspiring|uplifting|motivating|encouraging)\\b","\\binspire me\\b","\\bmotivate me\\b","\\bi need (?:some )?(?:motivation|inspiration|encouragement)\\b"],t.s)
B.bk6=s(["\\b(?:suicid\\w*|kill myself|end it|hopeless|worthless|depress\\w*)\\b"],t.s)
B.ap7=new A.X("something-inspiring",B.b_V,B.bk6,"Here is one from the Qur'an: with hardship comes ease, and the verse says it twice (Qur'an 94:5, Qur'an 94:6). The difficulty you are in is real, and the promise is that it is not the end of the story.\n\nAnother: Allah does not burden a soul beyond what it can bear (Qur'an 2:286).\n\nIf you would like more, the Stories screen has the lives of the prophets, and For Your Heart has verses and du'as for how you feel today.\n\nSource: Qur'an 94:5, 94:6, 2:286.\n[GO:/stories]\n[GO:/feelings]","encouragement",!0,"")
