B.bJh=new A.Q(B.bLV,["The Qur\u2019an asks for respect and fairness: \u201cThere shall be no compulsion in [acceptance of] the religion\u201d (Qur\u2019an 2:256), and to argue only \u201cin a way that is best\u201d (Qur\u2019an 16:125). Muslims believe Allah sent prophets to many peoples with one core message, the oneness of God, and they believe in all of those prophets (Qur\u2019an 3:84).","Christianity: Muslims share the love of Jesus and Mary, his virgin birth, his miracles and the Gospel he was given. Islam differs on who he is: \u201ca messenger of Allah and His word which He directed to Mary\u201d (Qur\u2019an 4:171), not the Son of God or part of a Trinity, and it teaches that he was not crucified (Qur\u2019an 4:157).","Judaism: Muslims share strict belief in one God, Abraham and Moses, and the Torah as revealed by Allah, \u201cin which was guidance and light\u201d (Qur\u2019an 5:44). Islam differs in accepting Jesus and then Muhammad \ufdfa as prophets, the last sent to all people (Qur\u2019an 33:40).","Hinduism: Encyclopaedia Britannica describes belief in brahman as ultimate reality, rebirth (samsara), karma and liberation (moksha). Islam differs on God, who is One and distinct from His creation (Qur\u2019an 112:1-4), and on the afterlife: one life, then resurrection, not rebirth (Qur\u2019an 23:100).","Buddhism: Britannica summarises its teaching as the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path, without a creator God at the centre. A Muslim can recognise its warnings about craving; Islam begins with Allah, who is near and answers (Qur\u2019an 2:186), and teaches resurrection rather than rebirth."],t.w)
